l,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-121","type":"text","x":16,"y":552,"width":670,"height":24,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":122,"version":1,"versionNonce":123,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"NOTE: not cross-checked against the official course outline","rawText":"NOTE: voice session said a sidebar screenshot was received","fontSize":12.5,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":12.5,"containerId":null,"originalText":"NOTE: not cross-checked against the official course outline","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-124","type":"text","x":700,"y":552,"width":980,"height":24,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":125,"version":1,"versionNonce":126,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"The six subtopics above follow the study guide's 3.1–3.6","rawText":"No screenshot reached me in text. Six subtopics above match the study guide's 3.1–3.6","fontSize":12.5,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":12.5,"containerId":null,"originalText":"The six subtopics above follow the study guide's 3.1–3.6","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-127","type":"text","x":1700,"y":552,"width":620,"height":24,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#b4590c","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":128,"version":1,"versionNonce":129,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"Compare with the official course outline and note any difference","rawText":"Please compare with your real sidebar and tell me any difference","fontSize":12.5,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":12.5,"containerId":null,"originalText":"Compare with the official course outline and note any difference","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-130","type":"text","x":0,"y":614,"width":2400,"height":36,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f3d8a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":131,"version":1,"versionNonce":132,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"CORE CONCEPTS — FULL DEPTH","rawText":"CORE CONCEPTS — FULL DEPTH","fontSize":26,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":26,"containerId":null,"originalText":"CORE CONCEPTS — FULL DEPTH","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-133","type":"rectangle","x":0,"y":662,"width":1150,"height":407.2,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":134,"version":1,"versionNonce":135,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-136","type":"text","x":16,"y":674,"width":1118,"height":24,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":137,"version":1,"versionNonce":138,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"01 · 3.1 — CLAUDE.md: WHAT IT IS AND WHERE IT LIVES (THE SCOPES)","rawText":"01 · 3.1 — CLAUDE.md: WHAT IT IS AND WHERE IT LIVES (THE SCOPES)","fontSize":18,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":18,"containerId":null,"originalText":"01 · 3.1 — CLAUDE.md: WHAT IT IS AND WHERE IT LIVES (THE SCOPES)","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-139","type":"text","x":16,"y":706,"width":1118,"height":343.2,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":140,"version":1,"versionNonce":141,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"WHAT: CLAUDE.md is a markdown file of standing instructions that Claude Code loads into context automatically. It is ADVICE that is always present (not a\nguarantee, not a check).\n\nTHE LOCATIONS (know which are shared through git):\n• MANAGED POLICY (org-wide, set by IT through MDM/Group Policy/Ansible): e.g. /etc/claude-code/CLAUDE.md on Linux. Cannot be excluded by users. Not in the\nrepo.\n• USER: ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md — your personal preferences across ALL projects. NEVER shared through version control.\n• PROJECT: ./CLAUDE.md or ./.claude/CLAUDE.md — committed to git, shared with the whole team.\n• LOCAL PROJECT: ./CLAUDE.local.md — personal notes for this repo; add it to .gitignore, not shared.\n• SUBDIRECTORY: path/to/dir/CLAUDE.md — committed, but loaded lazily (see 02).\n• USER RULES: ~/.claude/rules/*.md (personal, all projects). PROJECT RULES: .claude/rules/*.md (shared) — see panel 05.\n\n★ THE #1 TRAP: team standards written in ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md. They follow the individual, not the repo. One developer gets them; a new hire clones the repo\nand Claude uses the wrong test framework. FIX: move the standards to ./CLAUDE.md (or ./.claude/CLAUDE.md) and commit.\n\nEXAMPLE: 'We use Vitest, not Jest' belongs in the project CLAUDE.md. 'I prefer terse answers' belongs in ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md. 'My local DB port is 5433'\nbelongs in CLAUDE.local.md.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'new teammate doesn't get the conventions' → standards are in the user-level file. 'personal preference, shouldn't affect teammates' → user or\nlocal file.","rawText":"WHAT: CLAUDE.md is a markdown file of standing instructions that Claude Code loads into context automatically. It is ADVICE that is always present (not a\nguarantee, not a check).\n\nTHE LOCATIONS (know which are shared through git):\n• MANAGED POLICY (org-wide, set by IT through MDM/Group Policy/Ansible): e.g. /etc/claude-code/CLAUDE.md on Linux. Cannot be excluded by users. Not in the\nrepo.\n• USER: ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md — your personal preferences across ALL projects. NEVER shared through version control.\n• PROJECT: ./CLAUDE.md or ./.claude/CLAUDE.md — committed to git, shared with the whole team.\n• LOCAL PROJECT: ./CLAUDE.local.md — personal notes for this repo; add it to .gitignore, not shared.\n• SUBDIRECTORY: path/to/dir/CLAUDE.md — committed, but loaded lazily (see 02).\n• USER RULES: ~/.claude/rules/*.md (personal, all projects). PROJECT RULES: .claude/rules/*.md (shared) — see panel 05.\n\n★ THE #1 TRAP: team standards written in ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md. They follow the individual, not the repo. One developer gets them; a new hire clones the repo\nand Claude uses the wrong test framework. FIX: move the standards to ./CLAUDE.md (or ./.claude/CLAUDE.md) and commit.\n\nEXAMPLE: 'We use Vitest, not Jest' belongs in the project CLAUDE.md. 'I prefer terse answers' belongs in ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md. 'My local DB port is 5433'\nbelongs in CLAUDE.local.md.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'new teammate doesn't get the conventions' → standards are in the user-level file. 'personal preference, shouldn't affect teammates' → user or\nlocal file.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"WHAT: CLAUDE.md is a markdown file of standing instructions that Claude Code loads into context automatically. It is ADVICE that is always present (not a\nguarantee, not a check).\n\nTHE LOCATIONS (know which are shared through git):\n• MANAGED POLICY (org-wide, set by IT through MDM/Group Policy/Ansible): e.g. /etc/claude-code/CLAUDE.md on Linux. Cannot be excluded by users. Not in the\nrepo.\n• USER: ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md — your personal preferences across ALL projects. NEVER shared through version control.\n• PROJECT: ./CLAUDE.md or ./.claude/CLAUDE.md — committed to git, shared with the whole team.\n• LOCAL PROJECT: ./CLAUDE.local.md — personal notes for this repo; add it to .gitignore, not shared.\n• SUBDIRECTORY: path/to/dir/CLAUDE.md — committed, but loaded lazily (see 02).\n• USER RULES: ~/.claude/rules/*.md (personal, all projects). PROJECT RULES: .claude/rules/*.md (shared) — see panel 05.\n\n★ THE #1 TRAP: team standards written in ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md. They follow the individual, not the repo. One developer gets them; a new hire clones the repo\nand Claude uses the wrong test framework. FIX: move the standards to ./CLAUDE.md (or ./.claude/CLAUDE.md) and commit.\n\nEXAMPLE: 'We use Vitest, not Jest' belongs in the project CLAUDE.md. 'I prefer terse answers' belongs in ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md. 'My local DB port is 5433'\nbelongs in CLAUDE.local.md.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'new teammate doesn't get the conventions' → standards are in the user-level file. 'personal preference, shouldn't affect teammates' → user or\nlocal file.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-142","type":"rectangle","x":1190,"y":662,"width":1150,"height":390.04,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":143,"version":1,"versionNonce":144,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-145","type":"text","x":1206,"y":674,"width":1118,"height":24,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":146,"version":1,"versionNonce":147,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"02 · 3.1 — LOAD ORDER: CONCATENATION, PRIORITY, LAZY LOADING, /compact","rawText":"02 · 3.1 — LOAD ORDER: CONCATENATION, PRIORITY, LAZY LOADING, /compact","fontSize":18,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":18,"containerId":null,"originalText":"02 · 3.1 — LOAD ORDER: CONCATENATION, PRIORITY, LAZY LOADING, /compact","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-148","type":"text","x":1206,"y":706,"width":1118,"height":326.04,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":149,"version":1,"versionNonce":150,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"CONCATENATE, DON'T OVERRIDE: every applicable file is added to the session context. Files closer to the working directory appear LATER and so have effective\npriority.\n\nORDER AT LAUNCH (starting in foo/bar/): (1) managed-policy CLAUDE.md, (2) ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md + ~/.claude/rules/*, (3) /foo/CLAUDE.md, (4)\n/foo/bar/CLAUDE.md, (5) /foo/bar/CLAUDE.local.md (after CLAUDE.md in the same directory), (6) .claude/rules/*.md without 'paths'. Claude walks UP from the\nworking directory collecting CLAUDE.md and CLAUDE.local.md; order is filesystem root → working directory.\n\n★ LAZY LOADING: CLAUDE.md files in subdirectories BELOW the working directory do NOT load at launch. They load when Claude reads a file in that subtree.\n\nAFTER /compact: the project-root CLAUDE.md is re-injected from disk. Nested CLAUDE.md files reload only when Claude next touches their subtree.\n\nSIZE: everything in CLAUDE.md enters context in FULL at launch, every session. Keep each file lean — the study guide cites roughly 200 lines as a target —\nand split big content into .claude/rules/ (panel 05).\n\nEXAMPLE: Working in services/billing/ — the root CLAUDE.md is loaded at launch; services/billing/CLAUDE.md loads once Claude reads a file there;\napps/web/CLAUDE.md does not load at all.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'subdirectory instructions not applied yet' → lazy loading. 'conflicting rules' → Claude may pick arbitrarily; audit and make them non-\ncontradictory.","rawText":"CONCATENATE, DON'T OVERRIDE: every applicable file is added to the session context. Files closer to the working directory appear LATER and so have effective\npriority.\n\nORDER AT LAUNCH (starting in foo/bar/): (1) managed-policy CLAUDE.md, (2) ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md + ~/.claude/rules/*, (3) /foo/CLAUDE.md, (4)\n/foo/bar/CLAUDE.md, (5) /foo/bar/CLAUDE.local.md (after CLAUDE.md in the same directory), (6) .claude/rules/*.md without 'paths'. Claude walks UP from the\nworking directory collecting CLAUDE.md and CLAUDE.local.md; order is filesystem root → working directory.\n\n★ LAZY LOADING: CLAUDE.md files in subdirectories BELOW the working directory do NOT load at launch. They load when Claude reads a file in that subtree.\n\nAFTER /compact: the project-root CLAUDE.md is re-injected from disk. Nested CLAUDE.md files reload only when Claude next touches their subtree.\n\nSIZE: everything in CLAUDE.md enters context in FULL at launch, every session. Keep each file lean — the study guide cites roughly 200 lines as a target —\nand split big content into .claude/rules/ (panel 05).\n\nEXAMPLE: Working in services/billing/ — the root CLAUDE.md is loaded at launch; services/billing/CLAUDE.md loads once Claude reads a file there;\napps/web/CLAUDE.md does not load at all.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'subdirectory instructions not applied yet' → lazy loading. 'conflicting rules' → Claude may pick arbitrarily; audit and make them non-\ncontradictory.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"CONCATENATE, DON'T OVERRIDE: every applicable file is added to the session context. Files closer to the working directory appear LATER and so have effective\npriority.\n\nORDER AT LAUNCH (starting in foo/bar/): (1) managed-policy CLAUDE.md, (2) ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md + ~/.claude/rules/*, (3) /foo/CLAUDE.md, (4)\n/foo/bar/CLAUDE.md, (5) /foo/bar/CLAUDE.local.md (after CLAUDE.md in the same directory), (6) .claude/rules/*.md without 'paths'. Claude walks UP from the\nworking directory collecting CLAUDE.md and CLAUDE.local.md; order is filesystem root → working directory.\n\n★ LAZY LOADING: CLAUDE.md files in subdirectories BELOW the working directory do NOT load at launch. They load when Claude reads a file in that subtree.\n\nAFTER /compact: the project-root CLAUDE.md is re-injected from disk. Nested CLAUDE.md files reload only when Claude next touches their subtree.\n\nSIZE: everything in CLAUDE.md enters context in FULL at launch, every session. Keep each file lean — the study guide cites roughly 200 lines as a target —\nand split big content into .claude/rules/ (panel 05).\n\nEXAMPLE: Working in services/billing/ — the root CLAUDE.md is loaded at launch; services/billing/CLAUDE.md loads once Claude reads a file there;\napps/web/CLAUDE.md does not load at all.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'subdirectory instructions not applied yet' → lazy loading. 'conflicting rules' → Claude may pick arbitrarily; audit and make them non-\ncontradictory.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-151","type":"rectangle","x":0,"y":1097.2,"width":1150,"height":372.88,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":152,"version":1,"versionNonce":153,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-154","type":"text","x":16,"y":1109.2,"width":1118,"height":24,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":155,"version":1,"versionNonce":156,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"03 · 3.1 — @import AND MODULAR ORGANIZATION (AND WHAT IT DOESN'T DO)","rawText":"03 · 3.1 — @import AND MODULAR ORGANIZATION (AND WHAT IT DOESN'T DO)","fontSize":18,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":18,"containerId":null,"originalText":"03 · 3.1 — @import AND MODULAR ORGANIZATION (AND WHAT IT DOESN'T DO)","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-157","type":"text","x":16,"y":1141.2,"width":1118,"height":308.88,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":158,"version":1,"versionNonce":159,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"@import: writing @path/to/file inside any CLAUDE.md expands that file inline at session start. Use it to split a big CLAUDE.md into focused files.\n\nDETAILS: relative paths resolve from the IMPORTING FILE's location (not the working directory). Absolute and ~/ paths work. Imports can nest up to 5 hops.\nThe first time external imports appear in a project, an approval dialog lists them; declining keeps them disabled.\n\n★ IMPORTS DO NOT SAVE TOKENS. Every imported file loads in FULL at launch. They organize; they don't shrink. For true lazy loading use .claude/rules/ with\n'paths' (panel 05).\n\nMONOREPO EXAMPLE (root CLAUDE.md): 'API service: @services/api/STANDARDS.md · Web: @apps/web/STANDARDS.md · Shared types: @packages/types/STANDARDS.md ·\nPersonal: @~/.claude/my-project-instructions.md'. The ~/ import is how you carry personal instructions across git worktrees (a gitignored CLAUDE.local.md\nexists only in the worktree where it was created).\n\nAGENTS.md: Claude Code does NOT read AGENTS.md natively. Put @AGENTS.md at the top of CLAUDE.md (portable), or symlink (ln -s AGENTS.md CLAUDE.md on Unix).\n\nMONOREPO NOISE: claudeMdExcludes in .claude/settings.local.json lets you skip other teams' CLAUDE.md files.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'split a huge CLAUDE.md' → @imports or .claude/rules. 'reduce tokens' → NOT @import; use path-scoped rules. 'import path is relative to what?' →\nthe file containing the import.","rawText":"@import: writing @path/to/file inside any CLAUDE.md expands that file inline at session start. Use it to split a big CLAUDE.md into focused files.\n\nDETAILS: relative paths resolve from the IMPORTING FILE's location (not the working directory). Absolute and ~/ paths work. Imports can nest up to 5 hops.\nThe first time external imports appear in a project, an approval dialog lists them; declining keeps them disabled.\n\n★ IMPORTS DO NOT SAVE TOKENS. Every imported file loads in FULL at launch. They organize; they don't shrink. For true lazy loading use .claude/rules/ with\n'paths' (panel 05).\n\nMONOREPO EXAMPLE (root CLAUDE.md): 'API service: @services/api/STANDARDS.md · Web: @apps/web/STANDARDS.md · Shared types: @packages/types/STANDARDS.md ·\nPersonal: @~/.claude/my-project-instructions.md'. The ~/ import is how you carry personal instructions across git worktrees (a gitignored CLAUDE.local.md\nexists only in the worktree where it was created).\n\nAGENTS.md: Claude Code does NOT read AGENTS.md natively. Put @AGENTS.md at the top of CLAUDE.md (portable), or symlink (ln -s AGENTS.md CLAUDE.md on Unix).\n\nMONOREPO NOISE: claudeMdExcludes in .claude/settings.local.json lets you skip other teams' CLAUDE.md files.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'split a huge CLAUDE.md' → @imports or .claude/rules. 'reduce tokens' → NOT @import; use path-scoped rules. 'import path is relative to what?' →\nthe file containing the import.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"@import: writing @path/to/file inside any CLAUDE.md expands that file inline at session start. Use it to split a big CLAUDE.md into focused files.\n\nDETAILS: relative paths resolve from the IMPORTING FILE's location (not the working directory). Absolute and ~/ paths work. Imports can nest up to 5 hops.\nThe first time external imports appear in a project, an approval dialog lists them; declining keeps them disabled.\n\n★ IMPORTS DO NOT SAVE TOKENS. Every imported file loads in FULL at launch. They organize; they don't shrink. For true lazy loading use .claude/rules/ with\n'paths' (panel 05).\n\nMONOREPO EXAMPLE (root CLAUDE.md): 'API service: @services/api/STANDARDS.md · Web: @apps/web/STANDARDS.md · Shared types: @packages/types/STANDARDS.md ·\nPersonal: @~/.claude/my-project-instructions.md'. The ~/ import is how you carry personal instructions across git worktrees (a gitignored CLAUDE.local.md\nexists only in the worktree where it was created).\n\nAGENTS.md: Claude Code does NOT read AGENTS.md natively. Put @AGENTS.md at the top of CLAUDE.md (portable), or symlink (ln -s AGENTS.md CLAUDE.md on Unix).\n\nMONOREPO NOISE: claudeMdExcludes in .claude/settings.local.json lets you skip other teams' CLAUDE.md files.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'split a huge CLAUDE.md' → @imports or .claude/rules. 'reduce tokens' → NOT @import; use path-scoped rules. 'import path is relative to what?' →\nthe file containing the import.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-160","type":"rectangle","x":1190,"y":1097.2,"width":1150,"height":372.88,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":161,"version":1,"versionNonce":162,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-163","type":"text","x":1206,"y":1109.2,"width":1118,"height":24,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":164,"version":1,"versionNonce":165,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"04 · 3.1 — DIAGNOSING 'CLAUDE ISN'T FOLLOWING MY CLAUDE.md'; ADVICE vs ENFORCEMENT","rawText":"04 · 3.1 — DIAGNOSING 'CLAUDE ISN'T FOLLOWING MY CLAUDE.md'; ADVICE vs ENFORCEMENT","fontSize":18,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":18,"containerId":null,"originalText":"04 · 3.1 — DIAGNOSING 'CLAUDE ISN'T FOLLOWING MY CLAUDE.md'; ADVICE vs ENFORCEMENT","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-166","type":"text","x":1206,"y":1141.2,"width":1118,"height":308.88,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":167,"version":1,"versionNonce":168,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"DEBUG FLOW:\n1) Run /memory. It lists EVERY CLAUDE.md, CLAUDE.local.md and rules file loaded in this session. If a file isn't listed, Claude cannot see it.\n2) Not listed? Check it is in a loaded location. ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md applies only to YOUR sessions.\n3) Listed but ignored? Check the instructions are specific and non-conflicting. Contradictory rules let Claude pick arbitrarily.\n4) Path-scoped rules? Use the InstructionsLoaded hook to log when each file loads and why.\n\n★ CLAUDE.md IS ADVICE, NOT ENFORCEMENT. For must-always rules (e.g., never write to secrets/), use a HOOK (Domain 1.5) or permissions.deny. CLAUDE.md is\nprose Claude may interpret inconsistently — it is not a check (ties to the running notes: a spec is not verification).\n\nMANAGED POLICY: for org rules users cannot opt out of, use managed-policy CLAUDE.md plus managed settings.json.\n\n/init: generates a starter CLAUDE.md for a project (in a repo with AGENTS.md it incorporates it).\n\nEXAMPLE: A rule 'never edit files in migrations/' written in CLAUDE.md gets ignored once. Fix: a PreToolUse hook (or permissions.deny) that blocks edits\nthere.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'instructions loaded but occasionally violated, and a violation is serious' → hook/permission, not stronger wording. 'which command shows what's\nloaded' → /memory.","rawText":"DEBUG FLOW:\n1) Run /memory. It lists EVERY CLAUDE.md, CLAUDE.local.md and rules file loaded in this session. If a file isn't listed, Claude cannot see it.\n2) Not listed? Check it is in a loaded location. ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md applies only to YOUR sessions.\n3) Listed but ignored? Check the instructions are specific and non-conflicting. Contradictory rules let Claude pick arbitrarily.\n4) Path-scoped rules? Use the InstructionsLoaded hook to log when each file loads and why.\n\n★ CLAUDE.md IS ADVICE, NOT ENFORCEMENT. For must-always rules (e.g., never write to secrets/), use a HOOK (Domain 1.5) or permissions.deny. CLAUDE.md is\nprose Claude may interpret inconsistently — it is not a check (ties to the running notes: a spec is not verification).\n\nMANAGED POLICY: for org rules users cannot opt out of, use managed-policy CLAUDE.md plus managed settings.json.\n\n/init: generates a starter CLAUDE.md for a project (in a repo with AGENTS.md it incorporates it).\n\nEXAMPLE: A rule 'never edit files in migrations/' written in CLAUDE.md gets ignored once. Fix: a PreToolUse hook (or permissions.deny) that blocks edits\nthere.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'instructions loaded but occasionally violated, and a violation is serious' → hook/permission, not stronger wording. 'which command shows what's\nloaded' → /memory.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"DEBUG FLOW:\n1) Run /memory. It lists EVERY CLAUDE.md, CLAUDE.local.md and rules file loaded in this session. If a file isn't listed, Claude cannot see it.\n2) Not listed? Check it is in a loaded location. ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md applies only to YOUR sessions.\n3) Listed but ignored? Check the instructions are specific and non-conflicting. Contradictory rules let Claude pick arbitrarily.\n4) Path-scoped rules? Use the InstructionsLoaded hook to log when each file loads and why.\n\n★ CLAUDE.md IS ADVICE, NOT ENFORCEMENT. For must-always rules (e.g., never write to secrets/), use a HOOK (Domain 1.5) or permissions.deny. CLAUDE.md is\nprose Claude may interpret inconsistently — it is not a check (ties to the running notes: a spec is not verification).\n\nMANAGED POLICY: for org rules users cannot opt out of, use managed-policy CLAUDE.md plus managed settings.json.\n\n/init: generates a starter CLAUDE.md for a project (in a repo with AGENTS.md it incorporates it).\n\nEXAMPLE: A rule 'never edit files in migrations/' written in CLAUDE.md gets ignored once. Fix: a PreToolUse hook (or permissions.deny) that blocks edits\nthere.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'instructions loaded but occasionally violated, and a violation is serious' → hook/permission, not stronger wording. 'which command shows what's\nloaded' → /memory.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-169","type":"rectangle","x":0,"y":1498.08,"width":1150,"height":390.04,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":170,"version":1,"versionNonce":171,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-172","type":"text","x":16,"y":1510.08,"width":1118,"height":24,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":173,"version":1,"versionNonce":174,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"05 · 3.3 — PATH-SPECIFIC RULES: .claude/rules/ WITH paths GLOBS","rawText":"05 · 3.3 — PATH-SPECIFIC RULES: .claude/rules/ WITH paths GLOBS","fontSize":18,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":18,"containerId":null,"originalText":"05 · 3.3 — PATH-SPECIFIC RULES: .claude/rules/ WITH paths GLOBS","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-175","type":"text","x":16,"y":1542.08,"width":1118,"height":326.04,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":176,"version":1,"versionNonce":177,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"WHAT: .claude/rules/*.md files (discovered recursively). Each can have YAML frontmatter with a 'paths' list of globs. A path-scoped rule loads ONLY when\nClaude reads a file matching one of the globs. A rule with NO 'paths' loads unconditionally at launch (same priority as .claude/CLAUDE.md).\n\nEXAMPLE FILE (.claude/rules/tests.md):  ---  paths:  - \"**/*.test.ts\"  - \"**/*.test.tsx\"  ---  # Test conventions: use describe/it; one assertion per it;\nmock with vi.mock.\n\nGLOB BASICS: * matches within one path segment; ** recurses across directories. *.md = root only. **/*.ts = TS anywhere. src/**/* = everything under src/.\nsrc/components/*.tsx = direct children only. src/**/*.{ts,tsx} = brace expansion. terraform/**/* = everything under terraform/.\n\nYAML RULES: paths must be a YAML LIST. Patterns that start with * or { MUST BE QUOTED.\n\nWHY IT BEATS A SUBDIRECTORY CLAUDE.md for cross-cutting conventions: test files, Terraform files or API handlers are SPREAD across many directories. A path\nrule written once matches them all; a subdirectory CLAUDE.md would need duplicating in dozens of folders.\n\n★ THE HEURISTIC: does the convention follow file TYPE/PATTERN or file LOCATION? Pattern → path-scoped rule with a glob. Location → subdirectory CLAUDE.md.\n\nLAZY BENEFIT: scoped rules cost no context until a matching file is touched. User-level ~/.claude/rules/ load first, so project rules win conflicts.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'test files in every package must follow X' → .claude/rules with **/*.test.* glob. 'conventions only for one folder' → subdirectory CLAUDE.md.","rawText":"WHAT: .claude/rules/*.md files (discovered recursively). Each can have YAML frontmatter with a 'paths' list of globs. A path-scoped rule loads ONLY when\nClaude reads a file matching one of the globs. A rule with NO 'paths' loads unconditionally at launch (same priority as .claude/CLAUDE.md).\n\nEXAMPLE FILE (.claude/rules/tests.md):  ---  paths:  - \"**/*.test.ts\"  - \"**/*.test.tsx\"  ---  # Test conventions: use describe/it; one assertion per it;\nmock with vi.mock.\n\nGLOB BASICS: * matches within one path segment; ** recurses across directories. *.md = root only. **/*.ts = TS anywhere. src/**/* = everything under src/.\nsrc/components/*.tsx = direct children only. src/**/*.{ts,tsx} = brace expansion. terraform/**/* = everything under terraform/.\n\nYAML RULES: paths must be a YAML LIST. Patterns that start with * or { MUST BE QUOTED.\n\nWHY IT BEATS A SUBDIRECTORY CLAUDE.md for cross-cutting conventions: test files, Terraform files or API handlers are SPREAD across many directories. A path\nrule written once matches them all; a subdirectory CLAUDE.md would need duplicating in dozens of folders.\n\n★ THE HEURISTIC: does the convention follow file TYPE/PATTERN or file LOCATION? Pattern → path-scoped rule with a glob. Location → subdirectory CLAUDE.md.\n\nLAZY BENEFIT: scoped rules cost no context until a matching file is touched. User-level ~/.claude/rules/ load first, so project rules win conflicts.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'test files in every package must follow X' → .claude/rules with **/*.test.* glob. 'conventions only for one folder' → subdirectory CLAUDE.md.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"WHAT: .claude/rules/*.md files (discovered recursively). Each can have YAML frontmatter with a 'paths' list of globs. A path-scoped rule loads ONLY when\nClaude reads a file matching one of the globs. A rule with NO 'paths' loads unconditionally at launch (same priority as .claude/CLAUDE.md).\n\nEXAMPLE FILE (.claude/rules/tests.md):  ---  paths:  - \"**/*.test.ts\"  - \"**/*.test.tsx\"  ---  # Test conventions: use describe/it; one assertion per it;\nmock with vi.mock.\n\nGLOB BASICS: * matches within one path segment; ** recurses across directories. *.md = root only. **/*.ts = TS anywhere. src/**/* = everything under src/.\nsrc/components/*.tsx = direct children only. src/**/*.{ts,tsx} = brace expansion. terraform/**/* = everything under terraform/.\n\nYAML RULES: paths must be a YAML LIST. Patterns that start with * or { MUST BE QUOTED.\n\nWHY IT BEATS A SUBDIRECTORY CLAUDE.md for cross-cutting conventions: test files, Terraform files or API handlers are SPREAD across many directories. A path\nrule written once matches them all; a subdirectory CLAUDE.md would need duplicating in dozens of folders.\n\n★ THE HEURISTIC: does the convention follow file TYPE/PATTERN or file LOCATION? Pattern → path-scoped rule with a glob. Location → subdirectory CLAUDE.md.\n\nLAZY BENEFIT: scoped rules cost no context until a matching file is touched. User-level ~/.claude/rules/ load first, so project rules win conflicts.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'test files in every package must follow X' → .claude/rules with **/*.test.* glob. 'conventions only for one folder' → subdirectory CLAUDE.md.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-178","type":"rectangle","x":1190,"y":1498.08,"width":1150,"height":407.2,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":179,"version":1,"versionNonce":180,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-181","type":"text","x":1206,"y":1510.08,"width":1118,"height":24,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":182,"version":1,"versionNonce":183,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"06 · 3.2 — THE FOUR CUSTOMIZATION PRIMITIVES (WHICH ONE FOR WHICH JOB)","rawText":"06 · 3.2 — THE FOUR CUSTOMIZATION PRIMITIVES (WHICH ONE FOR WHICH JOB)","fontSize":18,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":18,"containerId":null,"originalText":"06 · 3.2 — THE FOUR CUSTOMIZATION PRIMITIVES (WHICH ONE FOR WHICH JOB)","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-184","type":"text","x":1206,"y":1542.08,"width":1118,"height":343.2,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":185,"version":1,"versionNonce":186,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"WHAT: Claude Code can be extended four ways. The exam asks you to pick the right one.\n\n• SLASH COMMAND — .claude/commands/foo.md. Triggered when the USER types /foo. Runs in the main context. For reusable interactive workflows (/review,\n/commit).\n• SKILL — .claude/skills/foo/SKILL.md (+ supporting files). Triggered by the user typing /foo OR by Claude auto-invoking it when the description matches.\nMain context by default; isolated with context: fork. For task-specific procedures.\n• SUBAGENT — .claude/agents/foo.md. Delegated by Claude or the user. ALWAYS isolated, with its own tools and model. For verbose research, parallel work,\nspecialists.\n• HOOK — hooks config. Fires on lifecycle events (PreToolUse, PostToolUse, UserPromptSubmit…). Shell/code that runs deterministically. For auto-lint,\nblocking protected paths.\n\nNOTE: custom commands were merged into skills. .claude/commands/deploy.md and .claude/skills/deploy/SKILL.md both create /deploy and behave the same. Skills\nare the recommended form (supporting files, invocation control, dynamic context, subagent execution). The exam may show either.\n\nALWAYS-ON vs ON-DEMAND: CLAUDE.md is ALWAYS loaded (cheap per rule, costly at scale). Skills are loaded ON DEMAND (heavier per use, free when unused).\n\nEXAMPLE: 'Run our PR checklist when I ask' → command/skill. 'Never touch secrets/' → hook. 'Use Vitest' → CLAUDE.md. 'Summarize a huge diff without flooding\nmy context' → skill with context: fork or an Explore subagent.\n\nEXAM TELLS: guaranteed behavior → hook. Standing advice → CLAUDE.md. Triggered procedure → command/skill. Isolated heavy work → subagent/fork.","rawText":"WHAT: Claude Code can be extended four ways. The exam asks you to pick the right one.\n\n• SLASH COMMAND — .claude/commands/foo.md. Triggered when the USER types /foo. Runs in the main context. For reusable interactive workflows (/review,\n/commit).\n• SKILL — .claude/skills/foo/SKILL.md (+ supporting files). Triggered by the user typing /foo OR by Claude auto-invoking it when the description matches.\nMain context by default; isolated with context: fork. For task-specific procedures.\n• SUBAGENT — .claude/agents/foo.md. Delegated by Claude or the user. ALWAYS isolated, with its own tools and model. For verbose research, parallel work,\nspecialists.\n• HOOK — hooks config. Fires on lifecycle events (PreToolUse, PostToolUse, UserPromptSubmit…). Shell/code that runs deterministically. For auto-lint,\nblocking protected paths.\n\nNOTE: custom commands were merged into skills. .claude/commands/deploy.md and .claude/skills/deploy/SKILL.md both create /deploy and behave the same. Skills\nare the recommended form (supporting files, invocation control, dynamic context, subagent execution). The exam may show either.\n\nALWAYS-ON vs ON-DEMAND: CLAUDE.md is ALWAYS loaded (cheap per rule, costly at scale). Skills are loaded ON DEMAND (heavier per use, free when unused).\n\nEXAMPLE: 'Run our PR checklist when I ask' → command/skill. 'Never touch secrets/' → hook. 'Use Vitest' → CLAUDE.md. 'Summarize a huge diff without flooding\nmy context' → skill with context: fork or an Explore subagent.\n\nEXAM TELLS: guaranteed behavior → hook. Standing advice → CLAUDE.md. Triggered procedure → command/skill. Isolated heavy work → subagent/fork.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"WHAT: Claude Code can be extended four ways. The exam asks you to pick the right one.\n\n• SLASH COMMAND — .claude/commands/foo.md. Triggered when the USER types /foo. Runs in the main context. For reusable interactive workflows (/review,\n/commit).\n• SKILL — .claude/skills/foo/SKILL.md (+ supporting files). Triggered by the user typing /foo OR by Claude auto-invoking it when the description matches.\nMain context by default; isolated with context: fork. For task-specific procedures.\n• SUBAGENT — .claude/agents/foo.md. Delegated by Claude or the user. ALWAYS isolated, with its own tools and model. For verbose research, parallel work,\nspecialists.\n• HOOK — hooks config. Fires on lifecycle events (PreToolUse, PostToolUse, UserPromptSubmit…). Shell/code that runs deterministically. For auto-lint,\nblocking protected paths.\n\nNOTE: custom commands were merged into skills. .claude/commands/deploy.md and .claude/skills/deploy/SKILL.md both create /deploy and behave the same. Skills\nare the recommended form (supporting files, invocation control, dynamic context, subagent execution). The exam may show either.\n\nALWAYS-ON vs ON-DEMAND: CLAUDE.md is ALWAYS loaded (cheap per rule, costly at scale). Skills are loaded ON DEMAND (heavier per use, free when unused).\n\nEXAMPLE: 'Run our PR checklist when I ask' → command/skill. 'Never touch secrets/' → hook. 'Use Vitest' → CLAUDE.md. 'Summarize a huge diff without flooding\nmy context' → skill with context: fork or an Explore subagent.\n\nEXAM TELLS: guaranteed behavior → hook. Standing advice → CLAUDE.md. Triggered procedure → command/skill. Isolated heavy work → subagent/fork.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-187","type":"rectangle","x":0,"y":1933.28,"width":1150,"height":390.04,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":188,"version":1,"versionNonce":189,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-190","type":"text","x":16,"y":1945.28,"width":1118,"height":24,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":191,"version":1,"versionNonce":192,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"07 · 3.2 — CUSTOM SLASH COMMANDS: SCOPE, FRONTMATTER, SUBSTITUTIONS","rawText":"07 · 3.2 — CUSTOM SLASH COMMANDS: SCOPE, FRONTMATTER, SUBSTITUTIONS","fontSize":18,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":18,"containerId":null,"originalText":"07 · 3.2 — CUSTOM SLASH COMMANDS: SCOPE, FRONTMATTER, SUBSTITUTIONS","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-193","type":"text","x":16,"y":1977.28,"width":1118,"height":326.04,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":194,"version":1,"versionNonce":195,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"SCOPE (the classic question):\n• .claude/commands/ → PROJECT scope, version-controlled, EVERY teammate gets it on clone/pull (e.g., /review, /security-review).\n• ~/.claude/commands/ → USER scope, personal only (e.g., /scratch).\n• A command everyone should have belongs in .claude/commands/ — NOT ~/.claude/commands/, NOT CLAUDE.md, NOT a made-up .claude/config.json.\n\nFRONTMATTER (all optional): description (shown in /help; drives auto-invocation; keep short) · argument-hint ([issue-number], shown at autocomplete) ·\nallowed-tools (pre-approves tools while the command runs, e.g. Bash(git:*)) · model (haiku | sonnet | opus | inherit) · disable-model-invocation: true\n(human-triggered only — use for side effects like /deploy).\n\nBODY SUBSTITUTIONS: $ARGUMENTS = the full argument string; $ARGUMENTS[N] or $N index arguments by position (current docs are 0-based, older docs used $1 for\nthe first — recognize both); !`cmd` runs a bash command at load time and inlines its output; @path inlines a file's contents.\n\nEXAMPLE (.claude/commands/review.md): frontmatter with description, argument-hint [pr-number-or-path], allowed-tools Read, Grep, Glob, Bash(git diff:*);\nbody: the diff via !`git diff origin/main...HEAD`, the checklist via @.claude/docs/review-checklist.md, and 'report severity, file:line, issue, fix'. Commit\nit and teammates get /review on pull.\n\nPERSONAL VARIANT TRICK: copy a shared command under a new name into ~/.claude/commands/ to customize it without changing what teammates get.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'share a command with the team' → .claude/commands/. 'only humans should trigger a deploy' → disable-model-invocation: true.","rawText":"SCOPE (the classic question):\n• .claude/commands/ → PROJECT scope, version-controlled, EVERY teammate gets it on clone/pull (e.g., /review, /security-review).\n• ~/.claude/commands/ → USER scope, personal only (e.g., /scratch).\n• A command everyone should have belongs in .claude/commands/ — NOT ~/.claude/commands/, NOT CLAUDE.md, NOT a made-up .claude/config.json.\n\nFRONTMATTER (all optional): description (shown in /help; drives auto-invocation; keep short) · argument-hint ([issue-number], shown at autocomplete) ·\nallowed-tools (pre-approves tools while the command runs, e.g. Bash(git:*)) · model (haiku | sonnet | opus | inherit) · disable-model-invocation: true\n(human-triggered only — use for side effects like /deploy).\n\nBODY SUBSTITUTIONS: $ARGUMENTS = the full argument string; $ARGUMENTS[N] or $N index arguments by position (current docs are 0-based, older docs used $1 for\nthe first — recognize both); !`cmd` runs a bash command at load time and inlines its output; @path inlines a file's contents.\n\nEXAMPLE (.claude/commands/review.md): frontmatter with description, argument-hint [pr-number-or-path], allowed-tools Read, Grep, Glob, Bash(git diff:*);\nbody: the diff via !`git diff origin/main...HEAD`, the checklist via @.claude/docs/review-checklist.md, and 'report severity, file:line, issue, fix'. Commit\nit and teammates get /review on pull.\n\nPERSONAL VARIANT TRICK: copy a shared command under a new name into ~/.claude/commands/ to customize it without changing what teammates get.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'share a command with the team' → .claude/commands/. 'only humans should trigger a deploy' → disable-model-invocation: true.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"SCOPE (the classic question):\n• .claude/commands/ → PROJECT scope, version-controlled, EVERY teammate gets it on clone/pull (e.g., /review, /security-review).\n• ~/.claude/commands/ → USER scope, personal only (e.g., /scratch).\n• A command everyone should have belongs in .claude/commands/ — NOT ~/.claude/commands/, NOT CLAUDE.md, NOT a made-up .claude/config.json.\n\nFRONTMATTER (all optional): description (shown in /help; drives auto-invocation; keep short) · argument-hint ([issue-number], shown at autocomplete) ·\nallowed-tools (pre-approves tools while the command runs, e.g. Bash(git:*)) · model (haiku | sonnet | opus | inherit) · disable-model-invocation: true\n(human-triggered only — use for side effects like /deploy).\n\nBODY SUBSTITUTIONS: $ARGUMENTS = the full argument string; $ARGUMENTS[N] or $N index arguments by position (current docs are 0-based, older docs used $1 for\nthe first — recognize both); !`cmd` runs a bash command at load time and inlines its output; @path inlines a file's contents.\n\nEXAMPLE (.claude/commands/review.md): frontmatter with description, argument-hint [pr-number-or-path], allowed-tools Read, Grep, Glob, Bash(git diff:*);\nbody: the diff via !`git diff origin/main...HEAD`, the checklist via @.claude/docs/review-checklist.md, and 'report severity, file:line, issue, fix'. Commit\nit and teammates get /review on pull.\n\nPERSONAL VARIANT TRICK: copy a shared command under a new name into ~/.claude/commands/ to customize it without changing what teammates get.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'share a command with the team' → .claude/commands/. 'only humans should trigger a deploy' → disable-model-invocation: true.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-196","type":"rectangle","x":1190,"y":1933.28,"width":1150,"height":407.2,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":197,"version":1,"versionNonce":198,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-199","type":"text","x":1206,"y":1945.28,"width":1118,"height":24,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":200,"version":1,"versionNonce":201,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"08 · 3.2 — SKILLS: LAYOUT, FRONTMATTER, context: fork, SKILL vs CLAUDE.md","rawText":"08 · 3.2 — SKILLS: LAYOUT, FRONTMATTER, context: fork, SKILL vs CLAUDE.md","fontSize":18,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":18,"containerId":null,"originalText":"08 · 3.2 — SKILLS: LAYOUT, FRONTMATTER, context: fork, SKILL vs CLAUDE.md","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-202","type":"text","x":1206,"y":1977.28,"width":1118,"height":343.2,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":203,"version":1,"versionNonce":204,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"LAYOUT: .claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md (required: frontmatter + instructions) plus optional supporting files (checklist.md, examples). Discovery levels:\nenterprise, user (~/.claude/skills/), project (.claude/skills/), plugin. Precedence: enterprise overrides personal, personal overrides project. Plugin\nskills are namespaced plugin-name:skill-name.\n\nSKILL FRONTMATTER (beyond command fields): name · description (what it does and WHEN to use — drives auto-invocation) · when_to_use (extra trigger phrases)\n· arguments (named args → $issue, $branch) · user-invocable (false hides from the / menu; Claude can still use it) · disable-model-invocation (true blocks\nauto-invocation) · context (fork) · agent (Explore | Plan | custom, for forked skills) · hooks (scoped to the skill) · paths (globs gating auto-invocation).\n\n★ context: fork — by default a skill runs INLINE and its verbose output (file listings, searches, thoughts) piles into the main context. With fork, the\nskill becomes a fresh SUBAGENT's prompt with its own tools; only its FINAL SUMMARY returns. FORK for verbose, exploratory or independent work (codebase\nanalysis, big-diff summaries). DON'T fork pure 'apply these conventions' skills — the subagent has guidelines but no task and returns empty.\n\nallowed-tools PRE-APPROVES tools while the skill is active; it does NOT block other tools (permission rules still apply).\n\nSKILL vs CLAUDE.md: universal standards active every session → CLAUDE.md. On-demand task procedure with supporting files → skill. Per-file-type convention →\npath-scoped rule (or skill with paths). Must-run-as-code → hook. Verbose discovery → skill with context: fork or Explore.\n\nEXAMPLE: pr-summary skill with context: fork and agent: Explore summarizes a PR without flooding your session.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'skill output floods my main conversation' → context: fork. 'loaded every session' → CLAUDE.md; 'only when relevant' → skill.","rawText":"LAYOUT: .claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md (required: frontmatter + instructions) plus optional supporting files (checklist.md, examples). Discovery levels:\nenterprise, user (~/.claude/skills/), project (.claude/skills/), plugin. Precedence: enterprise overrides personal, personal overrides project. Plugin\nskills are namespaced plugin-name:skill-name.\n\nSKILL FRONTMATTER (beyond command fields): name · description (what it does and WHEN to use — drives auto-invocation) · when_to_use (extra trigger phrases)\n· arguments (named args → $issue, $branch) · user-invocable (false hides from the / menu; Claude can still use it) · disable-model-invocation (true blocks\nauto-invocation) · context (fork) · agent (Explore | Plan | custom, for forked skills) · hooks (scoped to the skill) · paths (globs gating auto-invocation).\n\n★ context: fork — by default a skill runs INLINE and its verbose output (file listings, searches, thoughts) piles into the main context. With fork, the\nskill becomes a fresh SUBAGENT's prompt with its own tools; only its FINAL SUMMARY returns. FORK for verbose, exploratory or independent work (codebase\nanalysis, big-diff summaries). DON'T fork pure 'apply these conventions' skills — the subagent has guidelines but no task and returns empty.\n\nallowed-tools PRE-APPROVES tools while the skill is active; it does NOT block other tools (permission rules still apply).\n\nSKILL vs CLAUDE.md: universal standards active every session → CLAUDE.md. On-demand task procedure with supporting files → skill. Per-file-type convention →\npath-scoped rule (or skill with paths). Must-run-as-code → hook. Verbose discovery → skill with context: fork or Explore.\n\nEXAMPLE: pr-summary skill with context: fork and agent: Explore summarizes a PR without flooding your session.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'skill output floods my main conversation' → context: fork. 'loaded every session' → CLAUDE.md; 'only when relevant' → skill.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"LAYOUT: .claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md (required: frontmatter + instructions) plus optional supporting files (checklist.md, examples). Discovery levels:\nenterprise, user (~/.claude/skills/), project (.claude/skills/), plugin. Precedence: enterprise overrides personal, personal overrides project. Plugin\nskills are namespaced plugin-name:skill-name.\n\nSKILL FRONTMATTER (beyond command fields): name · description (what it does and WHEN to use — drives auto-invocation) · when_to_use (extra trigger phrases)\n· arguments (named args → $issue, $branch) · user-invocable (false hides from the / menu; Claude can still use it) · disable-model-invocation (true blocks\nauto-invocation) · context (fork) · agent (Explore | Plan | custom, for forked skills) · hooks (scoped to the skill) · paths (globs gating auto-invocation).\n\n★ context: fork — by default a skill runs INLINE and its verbose output (file listings, searches, thoughts) piles into the main context. With fork, the\nskill becomes a fresh SUBAGENT's prompt with its own tools; only its FINAL SUMMARY returns. FORK for verbose, exploratory or independent work (codebase\nanalysis, big-diff summaries). DON'T fork pure 'apply these conventions' skills — the subagent has guidelines but no task and returns empty.\n\nallowed-tools PRE-APPROVES tools while the skill is active; it does NOT block other tools (permission rules still apply).\n\nSKILL vs CLAUDE.md: universal standards active every session → CLAUDE.md. On-demand task procedure with supporting files → skill. Per-file-type convention →\npath-scoped rule (or skill with paths). Must-run-as-code → hook. Verbose discovery → skill with context: fork or Explore.\n\nEXAMPLE: pr-summary skill with context: fork and agent: Explore summarizes a PR without flooding your session.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'skill output floods my main conversation' → context: fork. 'loaded every session' → CLAUDE.md; 'only when relevant' → skill.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-205","type":"rectangle","x":0,"y":2368.48,"width":1150,"height":441.52000000000004,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":206,"version":1,"versionNonce":207,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-208","type":"text","x":16,"y":2380.48,"width":1118,"height":24,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":209,"version":1,"versionNonce":210,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"09 · 3.4 — PLAN MODE vs DIRECT EXECUTION: WHEN EACH FITS","rawText":"09 · 3.4 — PLAN MODE vs DIRECT EXECUTION: WHEN EACH FITS","fontSize":18,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":18,"containerId":null,"originalText":"09 · 3.4 — PLAN MODE vs DIRECT EXECUTION: WHEN EACH FITS","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-211","type":"text","x":16,"y":2412.48,"width":1118,"height":377.52000000000004,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":212,"version":1,"versionNonce":213,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"PLAN MODE: Claude reads files and runs read-only commands but CANNOT edit source. It proposes a plan; you approve before any change.\n\nHOW TO ENTER: Shift+Tab cycles default → acceptEdits → plan · claude --permission-mode plan (also works headless with -p) · permissions.defaultMode: \"plan\"\nin .claude/settings.json · prefix a prompt with /plan. When the plan is ready you can approve and auto-run, approve and review each edit, or keep planning.\nCtrl+G opens the plan in your editor.\n\nUSE PLAN MODE WHEN: the change spans many files or architectural seams (library migration touching 45+ files; monolith → microservices), there are several\nvalid approaches with real tradeoffs (Redis vs in-memory cache), the scope is unknown ('how big is this?'), or the blast radius is high (schema migrations,\nauth refactors).\n\nUSE DIRECT EXECUTION WHEN: a single-file bug with a clear stack trace, one validation check or log line, a mechanical refactor with a known target (rename\nacross N files, acceptEdits), or a plan already exists.\n\nWHY NOT ALWAYS PLAN: plan mode adds overhead (extra exploration tokens + review). Skip it for three-line changes.\n\n★ LINK TO RUNNING NOTES: plan mode solves AMBIGUITY of approach and scope. It does NOT solve edge-case CORRECTNESS — a reviewed plan still leaves edge-case\nbehavior unverified until something checks it (tests).\n\nEXAMPLE: 'Restructure our monolith into microservices' → plan mode (letting implementation reveal boundaries causes rework when a hidden dependency\nsurfaces). 'Fix this null check on line 42' → direct.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'large, multi-file, architectural, multiple approaches' → plan. 'small, well-scoped, one file' → direct.","rawText":"PLAN MODE: Claude reads files and runs read-only commands but CANNOT edit source. It proposes a plan; you approve before any change.\n\nHOW TO ENTER: Shift+Tab cycles default → acceptEdits → plan · claude --permission-mode plan (also works headless with -p) · permissions.defaultMode: \"plan\"\nin .claude/settings.json · prefix a prompt with /plan. When the plan is ready you can approve and auto-run, approve and review each edit, or keep planning.\nCtrl+G opens the plan in your editor.\n\nUSE PLAN MODE WHEN: the change spans many files or architectural seams (library migration touching 45+ files; monolith → microservices), there are several\nvalid approaches with real tradeoffs (Redis vs in-memory cache), the scope is unknown ('how big is this?'), or the blast radius is high (schema migrations,\nauth refactors).\n\nUSE DIRECT EXECUTION WHEN: a single-file bug with a clear stack trace, one validation check or log line, a mechanical refactor with a known target (rename\nacross N files, acceptEdits), or a plan already exists.\n\nWHY NOT ALWAYS PLAN: plan mode adds overhead (extra exploration tokens + review). Skip it for three-line changes.\n\n★ LINK TO RUNNING NOTES: plan mode solves AMBIGUITY of approach and scope. It does NOT solve edge-case CORRECTNESS — a reviewed plan still leaves edge-case\nbehavior unverified until something checks it (tests).\n\nEXAMPLE: 'Restructure our monolith into microservices' → plan mode (letting implementation reveal boundaries causes rework when a hidden dependency\nsurfaces). 'Fix this null check on line 42' → direct.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'large, multi-file, architectural, multiple approaches' → plan. 'small, well-scoped, one file' → direct.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"PLAN MODE: Claude reads files and runs read-only commands but CANNOT edit source. It proposes a plan; you approve before any change.\n\nHOW TO ENTER: Shift+Tab cycles default → acceptEdits → plan · claude --permission-mode plan (also works headless with -p) · permissions.defaultMode: \"plan\"\nin .claude/settings.json · prefix a prompt with /plan. When the plan is ready you can approve and auto-run, approve and review each edit, or keep planning.\nCtrl+G opens the plan in your editor.\n\nUSE PLAN MODE WHEN: the change spans many files or architectural seams (library migration touching 45+ files; monolith → microservices), there are several\nvalid approaches with real tradeoffs (Redis vs in-memory cache), the scope is unknown ('how big is this?'), or the blast radius is high (schema migrations,\nauth refactors).\n\nUSE DIRECT EXECUTION WHEN: a single-file bug with a clear stack trace, one validation check or log line, a mechanical refactor with a known target (rename\nacross N files, acceptEdits), or a plan already exists.\n\nWHY NOT ALWAYS PLAN: plan mode adds overhead (extra exploration tokens + review). Skip it for three-line changes.\n\n★ LINK TO RUNNING NOTES: plan mode solves AMBIGUITY of approach and scope. It does NOT solve edge-case CORRECTNESS — a reviewed plan still leaves edge-case\nbehavior unverified until something checks it (tests).\n\nEXAMPLE: 'Restructure our monolith into microservices' → plan mode (letting implementation reveal boundaries causes rework when a hidden dependency\nsurfaces). 'Fix this null check on line 42' → direct.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'large, multi-file, architectural, multiple approaches' → plan. 'small, well-scoped, one file' → direct.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-214","type":"rectangle","x":1190,"y":2368.48,"width":1150,"height":338.56,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":215,"version":1,"versionNonce":216,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-217","type":"text","x":1206,"y":2380.48,"width":1118,"height":24,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":218,"version":1,"versionNonce":219,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"10 · 3.4 — THE Explore SUBAGENT AND THE RECOMMENDED WORKFLOW","rawText":"10 · 3.4 — THE Explore SUBAGENT AND THE RECOMMENDED WORKFLOW","fontSize":18,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":18,"containerId":null,"originalText":"10 · 3.4 — THE Explore SUBAGENT AND THE RECOMMENDED WORKFLOW","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-220","type":"text","x":1206,"y":2412.48,"width":1118,"height":274.56,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":221,"version":1,"versionNonce":222,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"Explore SUBAGENT: built-in, READ-ONLY, backed by a small fast model. Tools: Glob, Grep, Read, Bash — no Write or Edit. Each run uses its OWN context window\nand returns only a SUMMARY. Use it directly, or via a skill with context: fork and agent: Explore.\n\nGOOD FOR: 'where is X defined?', 'how does Y work?', broad searches, multi-phase tasks — anything verbose that would flood the main context. Thoroughness\nlevels: quick, medium, very thorough.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS: it keeps discovery OUT of the main conversation, so the main context stays clean for the actual work (links to Domain 5 context management).\n\nRECOMMENDED WORKFLOW: EXPLORE → PLAN → EXECUTE → COMMIT. Use Explore for the survey, have Claude write the plan, review or edit it (Ctrl+G), approve into\nacceptEdits, then commit. Plan mode for investigation, direct execution for edits.\n\nWHEN A PLAN ALREADY EXISTS: you don't need plan mode again — execute directly.\n\nEXAMPLE: 'Understand how auth works across this repo before changing it' → Explore subagent (summary back), then plan mode for the change, then execute.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'exploration output is flooding the session' → Explore subagent / context: fork. 'read-only' → Explore cannot edit.","rawText":"Explore SUBAGENT: built-in, READ-ONLY, backed by a small fast model. Tools: Glob, Grep, Read, Bash — no Write or Edit. Each run uses its OWN context window\nand returns only a SUMMARY. Use it directly, or via a skill with context: fork and agent: Explore.\n\nGOOD FOR: 'where is X defined?', 'how does Y work?', broad searches, multi-phase tasks — anything verbose that would flood the main context. Thoroughness\nlevels: quick, medium, very thorough.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS: it keeps discovery OUT of the main conversation, so the main context stays clean for the actual work (links to Domain 5 context management).\n\nRECOMMENDED WORKFLOW: EXPLORE → PLAN → EXECUTE → COMMIT. Use Explore for the survey, have Claude write the plan, review or edit it (Ctrl+G), approve into\nacceptEdits, then commit. Plan mode for investigation, direct execution for edits.\n\nWHEN A PLAN ALREADY EXISTS: you don't need plan mode again — execute directly.\n\nEXAMPLE: 'Understand how auth works across this repo before changing it' → Explore subagent (summary back), then plan mode for the change, then execute.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'exploration output is flooding the session' → Explore subagent / context: fork. 'read-only' → Explore cannot edit.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"Explore SUBAGENT: built-in, READ-ONLY, backed by a small fast model. Tools: Glob, Grep, Read, Bash — no Write or Edit. Each run uses its OWN context window\nand returns only a SUMMARY. Use it directly, or via a skill with context: fork and agent: Explore.\n\nGOOD FOR: 'where is X defined?', 'how does Y work?', broad searches, multi-phase tasks — anything verbose that would flood the main context. Thoroughness\nlevels: quick, medium, very thorough.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS: it keeps discovery OUT of the main conversation, so the main context stays clean for the actual work (links to Domain 5 context management).\n\nRECOMMENDED WORKFLOW: EXPLORE → PLAN → EXECUTE → COMMIT. Use Explore for the survey, have Claude write the plan, review or edit it (Ctrl+G), approve into\nacceptEdits, then commit. Plan mode for investigation, direct execution for edits.\n\nWHEN A PLAN ALREADY EXISTS: you don't need plan mode again — execute directly.\n\nEXAMPLE: 'Understand how auth works across this repo before changing it' → Explore subagent (summary back), then plan mode for the change, then execute.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'exploration output is flooding the session' → Explore subagent / context: fork. 'read-only' → Explore cannot edit.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-223","type":"rectangle","x":0,"y":2838.0,"width":1150,"height":390.04,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":224,"version":1,"versionNonce":225,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-226","type":"text","x":16,"y":2850.0,"width":1118,"height":24,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":227,"version":1,"versionNonce":228,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"11 · 3.5 — ITERATIVE REFINEMENT: EXAMPLES, TEST-DRIVEN, INTERVIEW","rawText":"11 · 3.5 — ITERATIVE REFINEMENT: EXAMPLES, TEST-DRIVEN, INTERVIEW","fontSize":18,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":18,"containerId":null,"originalText":"11 · 3.5 — ITERATIVE REFINEMENT: EXAMPLES, TEST-DRIVEN, INTERVIEW","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-229","type":"text","x":16,"y":2882.0,"width":1118,"height":326.04,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":230,"version":1,"versionNonce":231,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"1) CONCRETE INPUT/OUTPUT EXAMPLES: when prose is ambiguous (extraction, transformation, formatting), give 2–3 input → expected-output pairs. Claude\ngeneralizes from examples better than from adjectives like 'friendly but professional'. Examples double as regression cases.\n\n2) TEST-DRIVEN ITERATION: have Claude write tests FIRST (happy path, edge cases, performance budgets), run them, share the failures, implement the minimum\nto turn one red test green, repeat, then refactor with the suite as a safety net. Tests give PRECISE, CHECKABLE feedback and measurable convergence — unlike\nanother round of interpreting prose.\n\n3) INTERVIEW PATTERN: in an unfamiliar domain, have Claude ASK questions before coding — about implementation, edge cases and tradeoffs — and consolidate\nthe answers into a written spec. It surfaces considerations you hadn't thought of. Use for caching (eviction, invalidation, failure modes, memory budget),\nretries, auth, billing, migrations. It is the remedy when up-front specs keep missing UNANTICIPATED failure modes.\n\nHOW THEY DIFFER: examples → ambiguity in what the output should look like. Tests → KNOWN edge cases you can encode as pass/fail. Interview → UNKNOWN\nunknowns.\n\nEXAMPLE: A migration script fails on rows with null dates. Give that exact failing input and expected output (or a failing test). Don't rewrite the whole\nscript or write 'handle nulls CAREFULLY!'.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'inconsistent outputs despite prose rules' → examples. 'edge cases keep slipping through' → tests. 'we don't know what we don't know' →\ninterview.","rawText":"1) CONCRETE INPUT/OUTPUT EXAMPLES: when prose is ambiguous (extraction, transformation, formatting), give 2–3 input → expected-output pairs. Claude\ngeneralizes from examples better than from adjectives like 'friendly but professional'. Examples double as regression cases.\n\n2) TEST-DRIVEN ITERATION: have Claude write tests FIRST (happy path, edge cases, performance budgets), run them, share the failures, implement the minimum\nto turn one red test green, repeat, then refactor with the suite as a safety net. Tests give PRECISE, CHECKABLE feedback and measurable convergence — unlike\nanother round of interpreting prose.\n\n3) INTERVIEW PATTERN: in an unfamiliar domain, have Claude ASK questions before coding — about implementation, edge cases and tradeoffs — and consolidate\nthe answers into a written spec. It surfaces considerations you hadn't thought of. Use for caching (eviction, invalidation, failure modes, memory budget),\nretries, auth, billing, migrations. It is the remedy when up-front specs keep missing UNANTICIPATED failure modes.\n\nHOW THEY DIFFER: examples → ambiguity in what the output should look like. Tests → KNOWN edge cases you can encode as pass/fail. Interview → UNKNOWN\nunknowns.\n\nEXAMPLE: A migration script fails on rows with null dates. Give that exact failing input and expected output (or a failing test). Don't rewrite the whole\nscript or write 'handle nulls CAREFULLY!'.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'inconsistent outputs despite prose rules' → examples. 'edge cases keep slipping through' → tests. 'we don't know what we don't know' →\ninterview.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"1) CONCRETE INPUT/OUTPUT EXAMPLES: when prose is ambiguous (extraction, transformation, formatting), give 2–3 input → expected-output pairs. Claude\ngeneralizes from examples better than from adjectives like 'friendly but professional'. Examples double as regression cases.\n\n2) TEST-DRIVEN ITERATION: have Claude write tests FIRST (happy path, edge cases, performance budgets), run them, share the failures, implement the minimum\nto turn one red test green, repeat, then refactor with the suite as a safety net. Tests give PRECISE, CHECKABLE feedback and measurable convergence — unlike\nanother round of interpreting prose.\n\n3) INTERVIEW PATTERN: in an unfamiliar domain, have Claude ASK questions before coding — about implementation, edge cases and tradeoffs — and consolidate\nthe answers into a written spec. It surfaces considerations you hadn't thought of. Use for caching (eviction, invalidation, failure modes, memory budget),\nretries, auth, billing, migrations. It is the remedy when up-front specs keep missing UNANTICIPATED failure modes.\n\nHOW THEY DIFFER: examples → ambiguity in what the output should look like. Tests → KNOWN edge cases you can encode as pass/fail. Interview → UNKNOWN\nunknowns.\n\nEXAMPLE: A migration script fails on rows with null dates. Give that exact failing input and expected output (or a failing test). Don't rewrite the whole\nscript or write 'handle nulls CAREFULLY!'.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'inconsistent outputs despite prose rules' → examples. 'edge cases keep slipping through' → tests. 'we don't know what we don't know' →\ninterview.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-232","type":"rectangle","x":1190,"y":2838.0,"width":1150,"height":355.72,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":233,"version":1,"versionNonce":234,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-235","type":"text","x":1206,"y":2850.0,"width":1118,"height":24,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":236,"version":1,"versionNonce":237,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"12 · 3.5 — BATCH vs SEQUENCE FIXES, AND THE 'FIXES THAT AREN'T FIXES'","rawText":"12 · 3.5 — BATCH vs SEQUENCE FIXES, AND THE 'FIXES THAT AREN'T FIXES'","fontSize":18,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":18,"containerId":null,"originalText":"12 · 3.5 — BATCH vs SEQUENCE FIXES, AND THE 'FIXES THAT AREN'T FIXES'","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-238","type":"text","x":1206,"y":2882.0,"width":1118,"height":291.72,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":239,"version":1,"versionNonce":240,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"BATCH vs SEQUENCE:\n• INTERACTING issues (fixing one changes the right answer for another — e.g., a date-format change affects parsing, display and DB writes) → send them in\nONE detailed message so Claude solves them together.\n• INDEPENDENT issues (a typo in a helper, a missing null check elsewhere) → fix SEQUENTIALLY, one per turn, for small diffs and attributable failures.\n• ONE failing edge case → give that specific input and expected output; don't rewrite everything.\n\nTHE 'FIXES THAT AREN'T FIXES' (from the author's running notes — same root pattern):\n• PLAN MODE for an edge-case correctness gap: plan mode scopes ambiguous approaches; a reviewed plan still leaves edge cases unverified.\n• CLAUDE.md for verification: it keeps rules in context but they remain prose Claude may interpret inconsistently. A specification is not a check.\n• EMPHASIS MARKERS (bold, IMPORTANT, CAPS, repetition): raise priority of a rule but don't resolve its ambiguity or create a feedback loop.\n• 'Use a bigger model' / 'add temperature 0': doesn't supply the missing concrete example or check.\nThe real fix is something that EXECUTES and reports pass/fail (tests, validation) or a concrete example.\n\nEXAMPLE: Edge-case bugs persist after three prose rewrites. Wrong: add 'IMPORTANT' in bold. Right: write failing tests for the edge cases and iterate until\ngreen.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'prose refinements already failed' → tests/examples, not more emphasis.","rawText":"BATCH vs SEQUENCE:\n• INTERACTING issues (fixing one changes the right answer for another — e.g., a date-format change affects parsing, display and DB writes) → send them in\nONE detailed message so Claude solves them together.\n• INDEPENDENT issues (a typo in a helper, a missing null check elsewhere) → fix SEQUENTIALLY, one per turn, for small diffs and attributable failures.\n• ONE failing edge case → give that specific input and expected output; don't rewrite everything.\n\nTHE 'FIXES THAT AREN'T FIXES' (from your running notes — same root pattern):\n• PLAN MODE for an edge-case correctness gap: plan mode scopes ambiguous approaches; a reviewed plan still leaves edge cases unverified.\n• CLAUDE.md for verification: it keeps rules in context but they remain prose Claude may interpret inconsistently. A specification is not a check.\n• EMPHASIS MARKERS (bold, IMPORTANT, CAPS, repetition): raise priority of a rule but don't resolve its ambiguity or create a feedback loop.\n• 'Use a bigger model' / 'add temperature 0': doesn't supply the missing concrete example or check.\nThe real fix is something that EXECUTES and reports pass/fail (tests, validation) or a concrete example.\n\nEXAMPLE: Edge-case bugs persist after three prose rewrites. Wrong: add 'IMPORTANT' in bold. Right: write failing tests for the edge cases and iterate until\ngreen.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'prose refinements already failed' → tests/examples, not more emphasis.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"BATCH vs SEQUENCE:\n• INTERACTING issues (fixing one changes the right answer for another — e.g., a date-format change affects parsing, display and DB writes) → send them in\nONE detailed message so Claude solves them together.\n• INDEPENDENT issues (a typo in a helper, a missing null check elsewhere) → fix SEQUENTIALLY, one per turn, for small diffs and attributable failures.\n• ONE failing edge case → give that specific input and expected output; don't rewrite everything.\n\nTHE 'FIXES THAT AREN'T FIXES' (from the author's running notes — same root pattern):\n• PLAN MODE for an edge-case correctness gap: plan mode scopes ambiguous approaches; a reviewed plan still leaves edge cases unverified.\n• CLAUDE.md for verification: it keeps rules in context but they remain prose Claude may interpret inconsistently. A specification is not a check.\n• EMPHASIS MARKERS (bold, IMPORTANT, CAPS, repetition): raise priority of a rule but don't resolve its ambiguity or create a feedback loop.\n• 'Use a bigger model' / 'add temperature 0': doesn't supply the missing concrete example or check.\nThe real fix is something that EXECUTES and reports pass/fail (tests, validation) or a concrete example.\n\nEXAMPLE: Edge-case bugs persist after three prose rewrites. Wrong: add 'IMPORTANT' in bold. Right: write failing tests for the edge cases and iterate until\ngreen.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'prose refinements already failed' → tests/examples, not more emphasis.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-241","type":"rectangle","x":0,"y":3256.04,"width":1150,"height":390.04,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":242,"version":1,"versionNonce":243,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-244","type":"text","x":16,"y":3268.04,"width":1118,"height":24,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":245,"version":1,"versionNonce":246,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"13 · 3.6 — HEADLESS CLAUDE CODE IN CI: -p, OUTPUT FORMATS, --json-schema","rawText":"13 · 3.6 — HEADLESS CLAUDE CODE IN CI: -p, OUTPUT FORMATS, --json-schema","fontSize":18,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":18,"containerId":null,"originalText":"13 · 3.6 — HEADLESS CLAUDE CODE IN CI: -p, OUTPUT FORMATS, --json-schema","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-247","type":"text","x":16,"y":3300.04,"width":1118,"height":326.04,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":248,"version":1,"versionNonce":249,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"THE FLAG: claude -p \"<prompt>\" (alias --print) runs ONE non-interactive query, prints to stdout and exits with a status code. No TTY check, no permission-\nprompt loop, no waiting on stdin. It is THE way to avoid CI hangs.\n\n★ TRAPS: CLAUDE_HEADLESS, --batch and </dev/null are NOT the unattended mode (distractors). Only -p.\n\n--output-format: text (default, for humans) · json (one object: final response, cost, duration, session ID, stop reason) · stream-json (newline-delimited\nevents; add --include-partial-messages for token deltas). --input-format stream-json lets a parent orchestrator feed structured events in.\n\n--json-schema <schema> (print mode): validates the final payload against your JSON Schema. Canonical use: the next CI step turns each finding into an inline\nPR comment. Typical schema: findings[] each with path, line, severity (info|warning|error|critical), category, message.\n\nOTHER USEFUL FLAGS: --model, --max-turns (cap loops), --max-budget-usd (cap spend). --bare skips hooks, plugins, MCP servers and auto-discovered CLAUDE.md\nfor fast, predictable CI (then load context explicitly with --append-system-prompt-file).\n\nEXAMPLE: claude -p \"Review changed files for security issues\" --output-format json --json-schema \"$(cat .ci/review-schema.json)\" --max-turns 8 >\nfindings.json\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'pipeline hangs waiting for input' → missing -p. 'need machine-parseable findings for inline comments' → --output-format json + --json-schema\n(not 'please reply in JSON').","rawText":"THE FLAG: claude -p \"<prompt>\" (alias --print) runs ONE non-interactive query, prints to stdout and exits with a status code. No TTY check, no permission-\nprompt loop, no waiting on stdin. It is THE way to avoid CI hangs.\n\n★ TRAPS: CLAUDE_HEADLESS, --batch and </dev/null are NOT the unattended mode (distractors). Only -p.\n\n--output-format: text (default, for humans) · json (one object: final response, cost, duration, session ID, stop reason) · stream-json (newline-delimited\nevents; add --include-partial-messages for token deltas). --input-format stream-json lets a parent orchestrator feed structured events in.\n\n--json-schema <schema> (print mode): validates the final payload against your JSON Schema. Canonical use: the next CI step turns each finding into an inline\nPR comment. Typical schema: findings[] each with path, line, severity (info|warning|error|critical), category, message.\n\nOTHER USEFUL FLAGS: --model, --max-turns (cap loops), --max-budget-usd (cap spend). --bare skips hooks, plugins, MCP servers and auto-discovered CLAUDE.md\nfor fast, predictable CI (then load context explicitly with --append-system-prompt-file).\n\nEXAMPLE: claude -p \"Review changed files for security issues\" --output-format json --json-schema \"$(cat .ci/review-schema.json)\" --max-turns 8 >\nfindings.json\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'pipeline hangs waiting for input' → missing -p. 'need machine-parseable findings for inline comments' → --output-format json + --json-schema\n(not 'please reply in JSON').","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"THE FLAG: claude -p \"<prompt>\" (alias --print) runs ONE non-interactive query, prints to stdout and exits with a status code. No TTY check, no permission-\nprompt loop, no waiting on stdin. It is THE way to avoid CI hangs.\n\n★ TRAPS: CLAUDE_HEADLESS, --batch and </dev/null are NOT the unattended mode (distractors). Only -p.\n\n--output-format: text (default, for humans) · json (one object: final response, cost, duration, session ID, stop reason) · stream-json (newline-delimited\nevents; add --include-partial-messages for token deltas). --input-format stream-json lets a parent orchestrator feed structured events in.\n\n--json-schema <schema> (print mode): validates the final payload against your JSON Schema. Canonical use: the next CI step turns each finding into an inline\nPR comment. Typical schema: findings[] each with path, line, severity (info|warning|error|critical), category, message.\n\nOTHER USEFUL FLAGS: --model, --max-turns (cap loops), --max-budget-usd (cap spend). --bare skips hooks, plugins, MCP servers and auto-discovered CLAUDE.md\nfor fast, predictable CI (then load context explicitly with --append-system-prompt-file).\n\nEXAMPLE: claude -p \"Review changed files for security issues\" --output-format json --json-schema \"$(cat .ci/review-schema.json)\" --max-turns 8 >\nfindings.json\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'pipeline hangs waiting for input' → missing -p. 'need machine-parseable findings for inline comments' → --output-format json + --json-schema\n(not 'please reply in JSON').","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-250","type":"rectangle","x":1190,"y":3256.04,"width":1150,"height":355.72,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":251,"version":1,"versionNonce":252,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-253","type":"text","x":1206,"y":3268.04,"width":1118,"height":24,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":254,"version":1,"versionNonce":255,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"14 · 3.6 — UNATTENDED PERMISSIONS & SESSION FLAGS IN CI","rawText":"14 · 3.6 — UNATTENDED PERMISSIONS & SESSION FLAGS IN CI","fontSize":18,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":18,"containerId":null,"originalText":"14 · 3.6 — UNATTENDED PERMISSIONS & SESSION FLAGS IN CI","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-256","type":"text","x":1206,"y":3300.04,"width":1118,"height":291.72,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":257,"version":1,"versionNonce":258,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"PERMISSION PROMPTS are the SECOND most common reason CI jobs hang (after forgetting -p). Options:\n• --allowedTools \"Read,Bash(git diff *),Bash(gh pr view *)\" — narrow allowlist, the preferred default.\n• --disallowedTools \"Edit,Write,Bash(rm *)\" — block writes for read-only review jobs.\n• --permission-mode acceptEdits or bypassPermissions — accept changes without prompts.\n• --dangerously-skip-permissions — only in sandboxed runners with no secrets.\n• --permission-prompt-tool <mcp-tool> — delegate approvals to an MCP tool that logs each call.\n\nSESSION FLAGS (tie to Domain 1.7): --session-id <uuid> pins an ID so later steps can resume · --resume <id> / -r continues a session (powers 'include prior\nfindings') · --continue / -c resumes the most recent in the cwd · --fork-session branches so retries don't mutate the canonical thread · --no-session-\npersistence writes nothing to disk (stateless runners).\n\nWHY A NARROW ALLOWLIST: least privilege — a review job that only needs to read a diff should not be able to run arbitrary shell commands.\n\nEXAMPLE: a read-only review job uses --allowedTools \"Read,Bash(git diff *)\" and --disallowedTools \"Edit,Write\"; never --dangerously-skip-permissions on a\nrunner holding secrets.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'job hangs on a permission prompt' → pre-approve the specific tools needed (allowedTools) rather than skipping all permissions.","rawText":"PERMISSION PROMPTS are the SECOND most common reason CI jobs hang (after forgetting -p). Options:\n• --allowedTools \"Read,Bash(git diff *),Bash(gh pr view *)\" — narrow allowlist, the preferred default.\n• --disallowedTools \"Edit,Write,Bash(rm *)\" — block writes for read-only review jobs.\n• --permission-mode acceptEdits or bypassPermissions — accept changes without prompts.\n• --dangerously-skip-permissions — only in sandboxed runners with no secrets.\n• --permission-prompt-tool <mcp-tool> — delegate approvals to an MCP tool that logs each call.\n\nSESSION FLAGS (tie to Domain 1.7): --session-id <uuid> pins an ID so later steps can resume · --resume <id> / -r continues a session (powers 'include prior\nfindings') · --continue / -c resumes the most recent in the cwd · --fork-session branches so retries don't mutate the canonical thread · --no-session-\npersistence writes nothing to disk (stateless runners).\n\nWHY A NARROW ALLOWLIST: least privilege — a review job that only needs to read a diff should not be able to run arbitrary shell commands.\n\nEXAMPLE: a read-only review job uses --allowedTools \"Read,Bash(git diff *)\" and --disallowedTools \"Edit,Write\"; never --dangerously-skip-permissions on a\nrunner holding secrets.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'job hangs on a permission prompt' → pre-approve the specific tools needed (allowedTools) rather than skipping all permissions.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"PERMISSION PROMPTS are the SECOND most common reason CI jobs hang (after forgetting -p). Options:\n• --allowedTools \"Read,Bash(git diff *),Bash(gh pr view *)\" — narrow allowlist, the preferred default.\n• --disallowedTools \"Edit,Write,Bash(rm *)\" — block writes for read-only review jobs.\n• --permission-mode acceptEdits or bypassPermissions — accept changes without prompts.\n• --dangerously-skip-permissions — only in sandboxed runners with no secrets.\n• --permission-prompt-tool <mcp-tool> — delegate approvals to an MCP tool that logs each call.\n\nSESSION FLAGS (tie to Domain 1.7): --session-id <uuid> pins an ID so later steps can resume · --resume <id> / -r continues a session (powers 'include prior\nfindings') · --continue / -c resumes the most recent in the cwd · --fork-session branches so retries don't mutate the canonical thread · --no-session-\npersistence writes nothing to disk (stateless runners).\n\nWHY A NARROW ALLOWLIST: least privilege — a review job that only needs to read a diff should not be able to run arbitrary shell commands.\n\nEXAMPLE: a read-only review job uses --allowedTools \"Read,Bash(git diff *)\" and --disallowedTools \"Edit,Write\"; never --dangerously-skip-permissions on a\nrunner holding secrets.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'job hangs on a permission prompt' → pre-approve the specific tools needed (allowedTools) rather than skipping all permissions.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-259","type":"rectangle","x":0,"y":3674.08,"width":1150,"height":407.2,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":260,"version":1,"versionNonce":261,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-262","type":"text","x":16,"y":3686.08,"width":1118,"height":24,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":263,"version":1,"versionNonce":264,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"15 · 3.6 — CI PATTERNS: SYNC vs BATCH, DEDUPE, INDEPENDENT REVIEWER, LARGE PRS","rawText":"15 · 3.6 — CI PATTERNS: SYNC vs BATCH, DEDUPE, INDEPENDENT REVIEWER, LARGE PRS","fontSize":18,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":18,"containerId":null,"originalText":"15 · 3.6 — CI PATTERNS: SYNC vs BATCH, DEDUPE, INDEPENDENT REVIEWER, LARGE PRS","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-265","type":"text","x":16,"y":3718.08,"width":1118,"height":343.2,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":266,"version":1,"versionNonce":267,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"PRE-MERGE (BLOCKING) vs OVERNIGHT: a check that BLOCKS a merge needs a real-time call. The Message Batches API (50% cheaper, up to ~24-hour latency) is for\nNON-blocking work like an overnight tech-debt report — never for the pre-merge gate.\n\nRE-REVIEW WITHOUT DUPLICATE COMMENTS: on a new push, feed the prior comments/findings back in and ask for only findings that are NEW or still UNADDRESSED\n(e.g., --resume the review session and attach the existing comments).\n\nTEST GENERATION: pass the existing test files so Claude doesn't re-cover scenarios already tested; document the runner, fixtures, mocking library and what a\n'valuable' test is in CLAUDE.md.\n\n★ INDEPENDENT REVIEWER: the session that WROTE the code carries its reasoning and is biased toward confirming its decisions. Never --continue from the\nimplementation session into review. Start a FRESH session (new --session-id) given only the diff and CLAUDE.md.\n\n★ LARGE PRs: 14 files in one pass → attention dilution and inconsistent verdicts on identical patterns. Do PER-FILE passes (local bugs, style, security) +\nONE INTEGRATION pass (cross-file concerns: API boundaries, schema migrations, contract drift, shared-state mutation). A bigger model or window does not fix\nattention allocation.\n\nEXAMPLE: PR re-pushed after fixes → the reviewer gets last round's comments and posts only new or unresolved ones, so the PR isn't spammed with repeats.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'merge gate' → synchronous. 'overnight report' → batch. 'duplicate comments' → pass prior findings. 'same session reviews its own code' →\nindependent session.","rawText":"PRE-MERGE (BLOCKING) vs OVERNIGHT: a check that BLOCKS a merge needs a real-time call. The Message Batches API (50% cheaper, up to ~24-hour latency) is for\nNON-blocking work like an overnight tech-debt report — never for the pre-merge gate.\n\nRE-REVIEW WITHOUT DUPLICATE COMMENTS: on a new push, feed the prior comments/findings back in and ask for only findings that are NEW or still UNADDRESSED\n(e.g., --resume the review session and attach the existing comments).\n\nTEST GENERATION: pass the existing test files so Claude doesn't re-cover scenarios already tested; document the runner, fixtures, mocking library and what a\n'valuable' test is in CLAUDE.md.\n\n★ INDEPENDENT REVIEWER: the session that WROTE the code carries its reasoning and is biased toward confirming its decisions. Never --continue from the\nimplementation session into review. Start a FRESH session (new --session-id) given only the diff and CLAUDE.md.\n\n★ LARGE PRs: 14 files in one pass → attention dilution and inconsistent verdicts on identical patterns. Do PER-FILE passes (local bugs, style, security) +\nONE INTEGRATION pass (cross-file concerns: API boundaries, schema migrations, contract drift, shared-state mutation). A bigger model or window does not fix\nattention allocation.\n\nEXAMPLE: PR re-pushed after fixes → the reviewer gets last round's comments and posts only new or unresolved ones, so the PR isn't spammed with repeats.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'merge gate' → synchronous. 'overnight report' → batch. 'duplicate comments' → pass prior findings. 'same session reviews its own code' →\nindependent session.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"PRE-MERGE (BLOCKING) vs OVERNIGHT: a check that BLOCKS a merge needs a real-time call. The Message Batches API (50% cheaper, up to ~24-hour latency) is for\nNON-blocking work like an overnight tech-debt report — never for the pre-merge gate.\n\nRE-REVIEW WITHOUT DUPLICATE COMMENTS: on a new push, feed the prior comments/findings back in and ask for only findings that are NEW or still UNADDRESSED\n(e.g., --resume the review session and attach the existing comments).\n\nTEST GENERATION: pass the existing test files so Claude doesn't re-cover scenarios already tested; document the runner, fixtures, mocking library and what a\n'valuable' test is in CLAUDE.md.\n\n★ INDEPENDENT REVIEWER: the session that WROTE the code carries its reasoning and is biased toward confirming its decisions. Never --continue from the\nimplementation session into review. Start a FRESH session (new --session-id) given only the diff and CLAUDE.md.\n\n★ LARGE PRs: 14 files in one pass → attention dilution and inconsistent verdicts on identical patterns. Do PER-FILE passes (local bugs, style, security) +\nONE INTEGRATION pass (cross-file concerns: API boundaries, schema migrations, contract drift, shared-state mutation). A bigger model or window does not fix\nattention allocation.\n\nEXAMPLE: PR re-pushed after fixes → the reviewer gets last round's comments and posts only new or unresolved ones, so the PR isn't spammed with repeats.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'merge gate' → synchronous. 'overnight report' → batch. 'duplicate comments' → pass prior findings. 'same session reviews its own code' →\nindependent session.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-268","type":"rectangle","x":1190,"y":3674.08,"width":1150,"height":338.56,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":269,"version":1,"versionNonce":270,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-271","type":"text","x":1206,"y":3686.08,"width":1118,"height":24,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":272,"version":1,"versionNonce":273,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"16 · 3.6 — CLAUDE.md IN CI, COST LEVERS & THE GITHUB ACTION","rawText":"16 · 3.6 — CLAUDE.md IN CI, COST LEVERS & THE GITHUB ACTION","fontSize":18,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":18,"containerId":null,"originalText":"16 · 3.6 — CLAUDE.md IN CI, COST LEVERS & THE GITHUB ACTION","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-274","type":"text","x":1206,"y":3718.08,"width":1118,"height":274.56,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":275,"version":1,"versionNonce":276,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"CLAUDE.md IN CI is loaded on EVERY invocation, so every line costs tokens on every run. Put the facts the model would otherwise guess: test runner command,\nfixture location, mocking library, what a valuable test is, review criteria (what blocks vs comment-only). Drop history; link ADRs; one declarative line\nbeats a paragraph. In --bare mode CLAUDE.md is NOT auto-loaded — opt in with --append-system-prompt-file.\n\nCOST LEVERS: PROMPT CACHING — stable content FIRST (system prompt, CLAUDE.md), dynamic content LAST (diff, prior findings); don't change model or tool list\nmid-session; cache reads are far cheaper than writes. BATCH API for non-blocking work (50% savings). MODEL BY JOB: a small fast model for lint/style, a mid\nmodel for hot-path security review, the biggest only for rare deep reviews (cap with --max-budget-usd). Switch with --model, not mid-session (invalidates\nthe cache).\n\nCLAUDE CODE GITHUB ACTION (anthropics/claude-code-action): inputs include anthropic_api_key (or OAuth/Bedrock/Vertex), prompt, claude_args (raw CLI args\nappended to claude -p, e.g. --max-turns, --model, --allowedTools), trigger_phrase (default @claude), label_trigger, track_progress, use_sticky_comment.\nGOTCHA: the token permissions must include pull-requests: write and issues: write or posting fails silently. Set model/turn caps via claude_args.\n\nEXAMPLE: a nightly job puts CLAUDE.md + conventions first and the changing diff last, to keep the cached prefix stable.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'costs too high on every CI run' → trim CLAUDE.md, order for caching, right-size the model, batch non-blocking work.","rawText":"CLAUDE.md IN CI is loaded on EVERY invocation, so every line costs tokens on every run. Put the facts the model would otherwise guess: test runner command,\nfixture location, mocking library, what a valuable test is, review criteria (what blocks vs comment-only). Drop history; link ADRs; one declarative line\nbeats a paragraph. In --bare mode CLAUDE.md is NOT auto-loaded — opt in with --append-system-prompt-file.\n\nCOST LEVERS: PROMPT CACHING — stable content FIRST (system prompt, CLAUDE.md), dynamic content LAST (diff, prior findings); don't change model or tool list\nmid-session; cache reads are far cheaper than writes. BATCH API for non-blocking work (50% savings). MODEL BY JOB: a small fast model for lint/style, a mid\nmodel for hot-path security review, the biggest only for rare deep reviews (cap with --max-budget-usd). Switch with --model, not mid-session (invalidates\nthe cache).\n\nCLAUDE CODE GITHUB ACTION (anthropics/claude-code-action): inputs include anthropic_api_key (or OAuth/Bedrock/Vertex), prompt, claude_args (raw CLI args\nappended to claude -p, e.g. --max-turns, --model, --allowedTools), trigger_phrase (default @claude), label_trigger, track_progress, use_sticky_comment.\nGOTCHA: the token permissions must include pull-requests: write and issues: write or posting fails silently. Set model/turn caps via claude_args.\n\nEXAMPLE: a nightly job puts CLAUDE.md + conventions first and the changing diff last, to keep the cached prefix stable.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'costs too high on every CI run' → trim CLAUDE.md, order for caching, right-size the model, batch non-blocking work.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"CLAUDE.md IN CI is loaded on EVERY invocation, so every line costs tokens on every run. Put the facts the model would otherwise guess: test runner command,\nfixture location, mocking library, what a valuable test is, review criteria (what blocks vs comment-only). Drop history; link ADRs; one declarative line\nbeats a paragraph. In --bare mode CLAUDE.md is NOT auto-loaded — opt in with --append-system-prompt-file.\n\nCOST LEVERS: PROMPT CACHING — stable content FIRST (system prompt, CLAUDE.md), dynamic content LAST (diff, prior findings); don't change model or tool list\nmid-session; cache reads are far cheaper than writes. BATCH API for non-blocking work (50% savings). MODEL BY JOB: a small fast model for lint/style, a mid\nmodel for hot-path security review, the biggest only for rare deep reviews (cap with --max-budget-usd). Switch with --model, not mid-session (invalidates\nthe cache).\n\nCLAUDE CODE GITHUB ACTION (anthropics/claude-code-action): inputs include anthropic_api_key (or OAuth/Bedrock/Vertex), prompt, claude_args (raw CLI args\nappended to claude -p, e.g. --max-turns, --model, --allowedTools), trigger_phrase (default @claude), label_trigger, track_progress, use_sticky_comment.\nGOTCHA: the token permissions must include pull-requests: write and issues: write or posting fails silently. Set model/turn caps via claude_args.\n\nEXAMPLE: a nightly job puts CLAUDE.md + conventions first and the changing diff last, to keep the cached prefix stable.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'costs too high on every CI run' → trim CLAUDE.md, order for caching, right-size the model, batch non-blocking work.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-277","type":"text","x":0,"y":4134.28,"width":2400,"height":36,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f3d8a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":278,"version":1,"versionNonce":279,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"THE THREE WAYS TO SHAPE CLAUDE CODE — ADVICE vs ON-DEMAND vs GUARANTEE","rawText":"THE THREE WAYS TO SHAPE CLAUDE CODE — ADVICE vs ON-DEMAND vs GUARANTEE","fontSize":26,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":26,"containerId":null,"originalText":"THE THREE WAYS TO SHAPE CLAUDE CODE — ADVICE vs ON-DEMAND vs GUARANTEE","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-280","type":"rectangle","x":0,"y":4182.28,"width":745,"height":215.44,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","backgroundColor":"#a5d8ff","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":281,"version":1,"versionNonce":282,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-283","type":"text","x":16,"y":4194.28,"width":713,"height":21,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":284,"version":1,"versionNonce":285,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"CLAUDE.md / RULES — ALWAYS-ON ADVICE","rawText":"CLAUDE.md / RULES — ALWAYS-ON ADVICE","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":15,"containerId":null,"originalText":"CLAUDE.md / RULES — ALWAYS-ON ADVICE","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-286","type":"text","x":16,"y":4223.28,"width":713,"height":154.44,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":287,"version":1,"versionNonce":288,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"WHAT: markdown instructions loaded into context.\nWHEN LOADED: CLAUDE.md at launch; path rules when matching files are touched.\nGUARANTEE? No — prose Claude may interpret inconsistently.\nBEST FOR: team standards, build commands, conventions.\nSCOPES: managed · user · project · local · subdirectory.\nCOST: all loaded text costs context every session.\nEXAMPLE: 'Use Vitest, not Jest.'\nMISTAKE: team rules in ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md; expecting enforcement.\nONE WORD: ADVICE","rawText":"WHAT: markdown instructions loaded into context.\nWHEN LOADED: CLAUDE.md at launch; path rules when matching files are touched.\nGUARANTEE? No — prose Claude may interpret inconsistently.\nBEST FOR: team standards, build commands, conventions.\nSCOPES: managed · user · project · local · subdirectory.\nCOST: all loaded text costs context every session.\nEXAMPLE: 'Use Vitest, not Jest.'\nMISTAKE: team rules in ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md; expecting enforcement.\nONE WORD: ADVICE","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"WHAT: markdown instructions loaded into context.\nWHEN LOADED: CLAUDE.md at launch; path rules when matching files are touched.\nGUARANTEE? No — prose Claude may interpret inconsistently.\nBEST FOR: team standards, build commands, conventions.\nSCOPES: managed · user · project · local · subdirectory.\nCOST: all loaded text costs context every session.\nEXAMPLE: 'Use Vitest, not Jest.'\nMISTAKE: team rules in ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md; expecting enforcement.\nONE WORD: ADVICE","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-289","type":"rectangle","x":775,"y":4182.28,"width":745,"height":215.44,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f8a7c","backgroundColor":"#b2f2bb","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":290,"version":1,"versionNonce":291,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-292","type":"text","x":791,"y":4194.28,"width":713,"height":21,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":293,"version":1,"versionNonce":294,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"COMMAND / SKILL / SUBAGENT — ON DEMAND","rawText":"COMMAND / SKILL / SUBAGENT — ON DEMAND","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":15,"containerId":null,"originalText":"COMMAND / SKILL / SUBAGENT — ON DEMAND","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-295","type":"text","x":791,"y":4223.28,"width":713,"height":154.44,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":296,"version":1,"versionNonce":297,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"WHAT: reusable procedures invoked when needed.\nWHEN LOADED: when the user types /name or Claude matches the description.\nGUARANTEE? No — still model-driven, but scoped and repeatable.\nBEST FOR: PR reviews, summaries, verbose analysis.\nLOCATIONS: .claude/commands · .claude/skills · .claude/agents (shared) or ~/.claude/… (personal).\nISOLATION: skill + context: fork or subagent keeps output out of main context.\nEXAMPLE: /review checklist.\nMISTAKE: putting a rarely used procedure in CLAUDE.md (loads every session).\nONE WORD: ON-DEMAND","rawText":"WHAT: reusable procedures invoked when needed.\nWHEN LOADED: when the user types /name or Claude matches the description.\nGUARANTEE? No — still model-driven, but scoped and repeatable.\nBEST FOR: PR reviews, summaries, verbose analysis.\nLOCATIONS: .claude/commands · .claude/skills · .claude/agents (shared) or ~/.claude/… (personal).\nISOLATION: skill + context: fork or subagent keeps output out of main context.\nEXAMPLE: /review checklist.\nMISTAKE: putting a rarely used procedure in CLAUDE.md (loads every session).\nONE WORD: ON-DEMAND","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"WHAT: reusable procedures invoked when needed.\nWHEN LOADED: when the user types /name or Claude matches the description.\nGUARANTEE? No — still model-driven, but scoped and repeatable.\nBEST FOR: PR reviews, summaries, verbose analysis.\nLOCATIONS: .claude/commands · .claude/skills · .claude/agents (shared) or ~/.claude/… (personal).\nISOLATION: skill + context: fork or subagent keeps output out of main context.\nEXAMPLE: /review checklist.\nMISTAKE: putting a rarely used procedure in CLAUDE.md (loads every session).\nONE WORD: ON-DEMAND","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-298","type":"rectangle","x":1550,"y":4182.28,"width":745,"height":215.44,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#8a4fd1","backgroundColor":"#eebefa","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":299,"version":1,"versionNonce":300,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-301","type":"text","x":1566,"y":4194.28,"width":713,"height":21,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":302,"version":1,"versionNonce":303,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"HOOK / PERMISSION — GUARANTEED CODE","rawText":"HOOK / PERMISSION — GUARANTEED CODE","fontSize":15,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":15,"containerId":null,"originalText":"HOOK / PERMISSION — GUARANTEED CODE","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-304","type":"text","x":1566,"y":4223.28,"width":713,"height":154.44,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":305,"version":1,"versionNonce":306,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"WHAT: code or rules that run deterministically.\nWHEN: at lifecycle events (PreToolUse, PostToolUse…).\nGUARANTEE? Yes.\nBEST FOR: must-always/never rules, auto-lint, blocking protected paths.\nALSO: permissions.deny, managed settings for org policy.\nCOST: you must write and maintain code.\nEXAMPLE: block writes to secrets/.\nMISTAKE: using CLAUDE.md or CAPS for a rule that must never be broken.\nONE WORD: GUARANTEE","rawText":"WHAT: code or rules that run deterministically.\nWHEN: at lifecycle events (PreToolUse, PostToolUse…).\nGUARANTEE? Yes.\nBEST FOR: must-always/never rules, auto-lint, blocking protected paths.\nALSO: permissions.deny, managed settings for org policy.\nCOST: you must write and maintain code.\nEXAMPLE: block writes to secrets/.\nMISTAKE: using CLAUDE.md or CAPS for a rule that must never be broken.\nONE WORD: GUARANTEE","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"WHAT: code or rules that run deterministically.\nWHEN: at lifecycle events (PreToolUse, PostToolUse…).\nGUARANTEE? Yes.\nBEST FOR: must-always/never rules, auto-lint, blocking protected paths.\nALSO: permissions.deny, managed settings for org policy.\nCOST: you must write and maintain code.\nEXAMPLE: block writes to secrets/.\nMISTAKE: using CLAUDE.md or CAPS for a rule that must never be broken.\nONE WORD: GUARANTEE","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-307","type":"text","x":0,"y":4427.719999999999,"width":2400,"height":36,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f3d8a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":308,"version":1,"versionNonce":309,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"★ DISCRIMINATORS — WHEN TWO ANSWERS LOOK RIGHT, THIS DECIDES IT","rawText":"★ DISCRIMINATORS — WHEN TWO ANSWERS LOOK RIGHT, THIS DECIDES IT","fontSize":26,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":26,"containerId":null,"originalText":"★ DISCRIMINATORS — WHEN TWO ANSWERS LOOK RIGHT, THIS DECIDES IT","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-310","type":"rectangle","x":0,"y":4475.719999999999,"width":1150,"height":268.92,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":311,"version":1,"versionNonce":312,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-313","type":"text","x":16,"y":4487.719999999999,"width":1118,"height":23,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":314,"version":1,"versionNonce":315,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"1 · TEAM STANDARD IN ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md  vs  PROJECT CLAUDE.md","rawText":"1 · TEAM STANDARD IN ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md  vs  PROJECT CLAUDE.md","fontSize":17,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":17,"containerId":null,"originalText":"1 · TEAM STANDARD IN ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md  vs  PROJECT CLAUDE.md","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-316","type":"text","x":16,"y":4518.719999999999,"width":1118,"height":205.92000000000002,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":317,"version":1,"versionNonce":318,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Should every teammate get this instruction?\n\nLOOK FOR → PROJECT: 'new hire gets the wrong framework', 'team convention', 'must be shared via the repo'. LOOK FOR → USER/LOCAL: 'my personal preference',\n'my local setup'.\n~/.claude/CLAUDE.md is never shared through version control.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: If the rule belongs to the codebase → ./CLAUDE.md or ./.claude/CLAUDE.md, committed. If it belongs to a person → user file; to a person in\none repo → CLAUDE.local.md (gitignored).\n\nEXAMPLE: 'Use Vitest' placed in the user file: the author gets Vitest, a new teammate gets Jest. Move it to the project file.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Leaving it in the user file or 'telling the new hire to copy the file' doesn't scale and silently drifts.","rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Should every teammate get this instruction?\n\nLOOK FOR → PROJECT: 'new hire gets the wrong framework', 'team convention', 'must be shared via the repo'. LOOK FOR → USER/LOCAL: 'my personal preference',\n'my local setup'.\n~/.claude/CLAUDE.md is never shared through version control.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: If the rule belongs to the codebase → ./CLAUDE.md or ./.claude/CLAUDE.md, committed. If it belongs to a person → user file; to a person in\none repo → CLAUDE.local.md (gitignored).\n\nEXAMPLE: 'Use Vitest' placed in the user file: the author gets Vitest, a new teammate gets Jest. Move it to the project file.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Leaving it in the user file or 'telling the new hire to copy the file' doesn't scale and silently drifts.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Should every teammate get this instruction?\n\nLOOK FOR → PROJECT: 'new hire gets the wrong framework', 'team convention', 'must be shared via the repo'. LOOK FOR → USER/LOCAL: 'my personal preference',\n'my local setup'.\n~/.claude/CLAUDE.md is never shared through version control.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: If the rule belongs to the codebase → ./CLAUDE.md or ./.claude/CLAUDE.md, committed. If it belongs to a person → user file; to a person in\none repo → CLAUDE.local.md (gitignored).\n\nEXAMPLE: 'Use Vitest' placed in the user file: the author gets Vitest, a new teammate gets Jest. Move it to the project file.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Leaving it in the user file or 'telling the new hire to copy the file' doesn't scale and silently drifts.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-319","type":"rectangle","x":1190,"y":4475.719999999999,"width":1150,"height":286.08000000000004,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":320,"version":1,"versionNonce":321,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-322","type":"text","x":1206,"y":4487.719999999999,"width":1118,"height":23,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":323,"version":1,"versionNonce":324,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"2 · SUBDIRECTORY CLAUDE.md  vs  .claude/rules WITH paths","rawText":"2 · SUBDIRECTORY CLAUDE.md  vs  .claude/rules WITH paths","fontSize":17,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":17,"containerId":null,"originalText":"2 · SUBDIRECTORY CLAUDE.md  vs  .claude/rules WITH paths","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-325","type":"text","x":1206,"y":4518.719999999999,"width":1118,"height":223.08,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":326,"version":1,"versionNonce":327,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Does the convention follow file LOCATION or file TYPE/PATTERN?\n\nLOOK FOR → SUBDIRECTORY: 'everything in services/billing follows X'. LOOK FOR → PATH RULE: 'all test files', 'all Terraform files', 'every *.tsx', 'spread\nacross many folders'.\nPattern across the tree → one glob rule. One folder → subdirectory file.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Ask: could these files live in many different directories? Yes → .claude/rules/*.md with paths globs. Is it one folder's rules? →\nsubdirectory CLAUDE.md.\n\nEXAMPLE: Test files live next to code in 40 packages: one rule with paths: **/*.test.tsx.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Duplicating a CLAUDE.md in 40 directories drifts and is hard to maintain; a root CLAUDE.md loads the rule every session even when no\ntest file is touched.","rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Does the convention follow file LOCATION or file TYPE/PATTERN?\n\nLOOK FOR → SUBDIRECTORY: 'everything in services/billing follows X'. LOOK FOR → PATH RULE: 'all test files', 'all Terraform files', 'every *.tsx', 'spread\nacross many folders'.\nPattern across the tree → one glob rule. One folder → subdirectory file.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Ask: could these files live in many different directories? Yes → .claude/rules/*.md with paths globs. Is it one folder's rules? →\nsubdirectory CLAUDE.md.\n\nEXAMPLE: Test files live next to code in 40 packages: one rule with paths: **/*.test.tsx.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Duplicating a CLAUDE.md in 40 directories drifts and is hard to maintain; a root CLAUDE.md loads the rule every session even when no\ntest file is touched.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Does the convention follow file LOCATION or file TYPE/PATTERN?\n\nLOOK FOR → SUBDIRECTORY: 'everything in services/billing follows X'. LOOK FOR → PATH RULE: 'all test files', 'all Terraform files', 'every *.tsx', 'spread\nacross many folders'.\nPattern across the tree → one glob rule. One folder → subdirectory file.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Ask: could these files live in many different directories? Yes → .claude/rules/*.md with paths globs. Is it one folder's rules? →\nsubdirectory CLAUDE.md.\n\nEXAMPLE: Test files live next to code in 40 packages: one rule with paths: **/*.test.tsx.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Duplicating a CLAUDE.md in 40 directories drifts and is hard to maintain; a root CLAUDE.md loads the rule every session even when no\ntest file is touched.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-328","type":"rectangle","x":0,"y":4785.799999999999,"width":1150,"height":234.6,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":329,"version":1,"versionNonce":330,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-331","type":"text","x":16,"y":4797.799999999999,"width":1118,"height":23,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":332,"version":1,"versionNonce":333,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"3 · CLAUDE.md RULE  vs  HOOK / permissions.deny","rawText":"3 · CLAUDE.md RULE  vs  HOOK / permissions.deny","fontSize":17,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":17,"containerId":null,"originalText":"3 · CLAUDE.md RULE  vs  HOOK / permissions.deny","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-334","type":"text","x":16,"y":4828.799999999999,"width":1118,"height":171.6,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":335,"version":1,"versionNonce":336,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Is this advice, or must it ALWAYS hold?\n\nLOOK FOR → HOOK/DENY: 'must never', 'protected paths', 'compliance', 'was violated again'. LOOK FOR → CLAUDE.md: 'preferred style', 'build command'.\nCLAUDE.md is advisory context, not enforcement.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: If a single violation is a serious incident → hook or permissions.deny. Otherwise CLAUDE.md is fine.\n\nEXAMPLE: 'Never write to secrets/' in CLAUDE.md still gets violated occasionally. Add a PreToolUse hook or permissions.deny.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Stronger wording or CAPS in CLAUDE.md remains probabilistic.","rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Is this advice, or must it ALWAYS hold?\n\nLOOK FOR → HOOK/DENY: 'must never', 'protected paths', 'compliance', 'was violated again'. LOOK FOR → CLAUDE.md: 'preferred style', 'build command'.\nCLAUDE.md is advisory context, not enforcement.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: If a single violation is a serious incident → hook or permissions.deny. Otherwise CLAUDE.md is fine.\n\nEXAMPLE: 'Never write to secrets/' in CLAUDE.md still gets violated occasionally. Add a PreToolUse hook or permissions.deny.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Stronger wording or CAPS in CLAUDE.md remains probabilistic.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Is this advice, or must it ALWAYS hold?\n\nLOOK FOR → HOOK/DENY: 'must never', 'protected paths', 'compliance', 'was violated again'. LOOK FOR → CLAUDE.md: 'preferred style', 'build command'.\nCLAUDE.md is advisory context, not enforcement.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: If a single violation is a serious incident → hook or permissions.deny. Otherwise CLAUDE.md is fine.\n\nEXAMPLE: 'Never write to secrets/' in CLAUDE.md still gets violated occasionally. Add a PreToolUse hook or permissions.deny.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Stronger wording or CAPS in CLAUDE.md remains probabilistic.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-337","type":"rectangle","x":1190,"y":4785.799999999999,"width":1150,"height":234.6,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":338,"version":1,"versionNonce":339,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-340","type":"text","x":1206,"y":4797.799999999999,"width":1118,"height":23,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":341,"version":1,"versionNonce":342,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"4 · @import  vs  .claude/rules (LAZY)","rawText":"4 · @import  vs  .claude/rules (LAZY)","fontSize":17,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":17,"containerId":null,"originalText":"4 · @import  vs  .claude/rules (LAZY)","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-343","type":"text","x":1206,"y":4828.799999999999,"width":1118,"height":171.6,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":344,"version":1,"versionNonce":345,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Are you trying to ORGANIZE or to SAVE TOKENS?\n\nLOOK FOR → @import: 'split a huge file into readable pieces'. LOOK FOR → RULES: 'only load when relevant', 'reduce context usage'.\n@imports load in full at launch.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Organization only → @import. Context savings → path-scoped rules.\n\nEXAMPLE: A 2,000-line CLAUDE.md split into five @imported files still loads 2,000 lines. Convert the file-type-specific parts into path-scoped rules.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Assuming imports are lazy leaves the token cost unchanged.","rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Are you trying to ORGANIZE or to SAVE TOKENS?\n\nLOOK FOR → @import: 'split a huge file into readable pieces'. LOOK FOR → RULES: 'only load when relevant', 'reduce context usage'.\n@imports load in full at launch.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Organization only → @import. Context savings → path-scoped rules.\n\nEXAMPLE: A 2,000-line CLAUDE.md split into five @imported files still loads 2,000 lines. Convert the file-type-specific parts into path-scoped rules.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Assuming imports are lazy leaves the token cost unchanged.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Are you trying to ORGANIZE or to SAVE TOKENS?\n\nLOOK FOR → @import: 'split a huge file into readable pieces'. LOOK FOR → RULES: 'only load when relevant', 'reduce context usage'.\n@imports load in full at launch.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Organization only → @import. Context savings → path-scoped rules.\n\nEXAMPLE: A 2,000-line CLAUDE.md split into five @imported files still loads 2,000 lines. Convert the file-type-specific parts into path-scoped rules.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Assuming imports are lazy leaves the token cost unchanged.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-346","type":"rectangle","x":0,"y":5044.4,"width":1150,"height":234.6,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":347,"version":1,"versionNonce":348,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-349","type":"text","x":16,"y":5056.4,"width":1118,"height":23,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":350,"version":1,"versionNonce":351,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"5 · PROJECT COMMAND  vs  USER COMMAND  vs  CLAUDE.md","rawText":"5 · PROJECT COMMAND  vs  USER COMMAND  vs  CLAUDE.md","fontSize":17,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":17,"containerId":null,"originalText":"5 · PROJECT COMMAND  vs  USER COMMAND  vs  CLAUDE.md","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-352","type":"text","x":16,"y":5087.4,"width":1118,"height":171.6,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":353,"version":1,"versionNonce":354,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Who should get this command?\n\nLOOK FOR → PROJECT: 'every developer gets it on clone/pull'. LOOK FOR → USER: 'personal', 'my own shortcut'.\nCLAUDE.md is for standing advice, not a command mechanism.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Shared → .claude/commands/ (or .claude/skills/). Personal → ~/.claude/commands/ (or ~/.claude/skills/).\n\nEXAMPLE: A /review command for the team: .claude/commands/review.md committed to the repo.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: ~/.claude/commands/ reaches only you; CLAUDE.md can't create a slash command; there is no .claude/config.json for this.","rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Who should get this command?\n\nLOOK FOR → PROJECT: 'every developer gets it on clone/pull'. LOOK FOR → USER: 'personal', 'my own shortcut'.\nCLAUDE.md is for standing advice, not a command mechanism.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Shared → .claude/commands/ (or .claude/skills/). Personal → ~/.claude/commands/ (or ~/.claude/skills/).\n\nEXAMPLE: A /review command for the team: .claude/commands/review.md committed to the repo.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: ~/.claude/commands/ reaches only you; CLAUDE.md can't create a slash command; there is no .claude/config.json for this.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Who should get this command?\n\nLOOK FOR → PROJECT: 'every developer gets it on clone/pull'. LOOK FOR → USER: 'personal', 'my own shortcut'.\nCLAUDE.md is for standing advice, not a command mechanism.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Shared → .claude/commands/ (or .claude/skills/). Personal → ~/.claude/commands/ (or ~/.claude/skills/).\n\nEXAMPLE: A /review command for the team: .claude/commands/review.md committed to the repo.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: ~/.claude/commands/ reaches only you; CLAUDE.md can't create a slash command; there is no .claude/config.json for this.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-355","type":"rectangle","x":1190,"y":5044.4,"width":1150,"height":234.6,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":356,"version":1,"versionNonce":357,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-358","type":"text","x":1206,"y":5056.4,"width":1118,"height":23,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":359,"version":1,"versionNonce":360,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"6 · SKILL  vs  CLAUDE.md","rawText":"6 · SKILL  vs  CLAUDE.md","fontSize":17,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":17,"containerId":null,"originalText":"6 · SKILL  vs  CLAUDE.md","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-361","type":"text","x":1206,"y":5087.4,"width":1118,"height":171.6,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":362,"version":1,"versionNonce":363,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Should it be loaded every session or only when needed?\n\nLOOK FOR → CLAUDE.md: 'universal standards', 'always active'. LOOK FOR → SKILL: 'task-specific procedure', 'supporting files', 'only for PR review'.\nCLAUDE.md is always-on; skills are on demand.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Needed in nearly every session → CLAUDE.md. Needed occasionally for a specific task → skill.\n\nEXAMPLE: A 300-line release checklist used twice a month → skill, not CLAUDE.md.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Putting it in CLAUDE.md costs context every session; making a universal rule a skill risks it not being applied.","rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Should it be loaded every session or only when needed?\n\nLOOK FOR → CLAUDE.md: 'universal standards', 'always active'. LOOK FOR → SKILL: 'task-specific procedure', 'supporting files', 'only for PR review'.\nCLAUDE.md is always-on; skills are on demand.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Needed in nearly every session → CLAUDE.md. Needed occasionally for a specific task → skill.\n\nEXAMPLE: A 300-line release checklist used twice a month → skill, not CLAUDE.md.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Putting it in CLAUDE.md costs context every session; making a universal rule a skill risks it not being applied.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Should it be loaded every session or only when needed?\n\nLOOK FOR → CLAUDE.md: 'universal standards', 'always active'. LOOK FOR → SKILL: 'task-specific procedure', 'supporting files', 'only for PR review'.\nCLAUDE.md is always-on; skills are on demand.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Needed in nearly every session → CLAUDE.md. Needed occasionally for a specific task → skill.\n\nEXAMPLE: A 300-line release checklist used twice a month → skill, not CLAUDE.md.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Putting it in CLAUDE.md costs context every session; making a universal rule a skill risks it not being applied.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-364","type":"rectangle","x":0,"y":5303.0,"width":1150,"height":251.76000000000002,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":365,"version":1,"versionNonce":366,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-367","type":"text","x":16,"y":5315.0,"width":1118,"height":23,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":368,"version":1,"versionNonce":369,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"7 · SKILL INLINE  vs  context: fork","rawText":"7 · SKILL INLINE  vs  context: fork","fontSize":17,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":17,"containerId":null,"originalText":"7 · SKILL INLINE  vs  context: fork","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-370","type":"text","x":16,"y":5346.0,"width":1118,"height":188.76000000000002,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":371,"version":1,"versionNonce":372,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Will the skill produce lots of intermediate output?\n\nLOOK FOR → FORK: 'verbose', 'exploratory', 'analysis of a large codebase/diff', 'floods context'. LOOK FOR → INLINE: 'apply these conventions', 'short\nprocedure the main session must act on'.\nFork returns only a summary.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Verbose or independent → context: fork (+ agent: Explore if read-only). Guidance-only skill → inline.\n\nEXAMPLE: pr-summary reads a huge diff → fork + Explore. A 'follow our naming rules' skill → inline (forking gives the subagent no task).\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Inline for verbose work clutters the main context; forking a guidelines-only skill returns nothing useful.","rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Will the skill produce lots of intermediate output?\n\nLOOK FOR → FORK: 'verbose', 'exploratory', 'analysis of a large codebase/diff', 'floods context'. LOOK FOR → INLINE: 'apply these conventions', 'short\nprocedure the main session must act on'.\nFork returns only a summary.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Verbose or independent → context: fork (+ agent: Explore if read-only). Guidance-only skill → inline.\n\nEXAMPLE: pr-summary reads a huge diff → fork + Explore. A 'follow our naming rules' skill → inline (forking gives the subagent no task).\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Inline for verbose work clutters the main context; forking a guidelines-only skill returns nothing useful.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Will the skill produce lots of intermediate output?\n\nLOOK FOR → FORK: 'verbose', 'exploratory', 'analysis of a large codebase/diff', 'floods context'. LOOK FOR → INLINE: 'apply these conventions', 'short\nprocedure the main session must act on'.\nFork returns only a summary.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Verbose or independent → context: fork (+ agent: Explore if read-only). Guidance-only skill → inline.\n\nEXAMPLE: pr-summary reads a huge diff → fork + Explore. A 'follow our naming rules' skill → inline (forking gives the subagent no task).\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Inline for verbose work clutters the main context; forking a guidelines-only skill returns nothing useful.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-373","type":"rectangle","x":1190,"y":5303.0,"width":1150,"height":234.6,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":374,"version":1,"versionNonce":375,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-376","type":"text","x":1206,"y":5315.0,"width":1118,"height":23,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":377,"version":1,"versionNonce":378,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"8 · allowed-tools  vs  BLOCKING TOOLS","rawText":"8 · allowed-tools  vs  BLOCKING TOOLS","fontSize":17,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":17,"containerId":null,"originalText":"8 · allowed-tools  vs  BLOCKING TOOLS","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-379","type":"text","x":1206,"y":5346.0,"width":1118,"height":171.6,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":380,"version":1,"versionNonce":381,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Are you pre-approving or restricting?\n\nLOOK FOR → allowed-tools: 'let it run git diff without prompts'. LOOK FOR → BLOCK: 'must not be able to write', permissions deny rules, --disallowedTools.\nallowed-tools pre-approves; it does NOT block other tools.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: To remove capability use deny rules/disallowed tools; to avoid prompts use allowed tools.\n\nEXAMPLE: A read-only review skill lists Read, Grep and Bash(git diff:*) as allowed AND denies Edit/Write elsewhere.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Assuming allowed-tools is a whitelist that blocks everything else leaves other tools available under normal permissions.","rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Are you pre-approving or restricting?\n\nLOOK FOR → allowed-tools: 'let it run git diff without prompts'. LOOK FOR → BLOCK: 'must not be able to write', permissions deny rules, --disallowedTools.\nallowed-tools pre-approves; it does NOT block other tools.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: To remove capability use deny rules/disallowed tools; to avoid prompts use allowed tools.\n\nEXAMPLE: A read-only review skill lists Read, Grep and Bash(git diff:*) as allowed AND denies Edit/Write elsewhere.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Assuming allowed-tools is a whitelist that blocks everything else leaves other tools available under normal permissions.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Are you pre-approving or restricting?\n\nLOOK FOR → allowed-tools: 'let it run git diff without prompts'. LOOK FOR → BLOCK: 'must not be able to write', permissions deny rules, --disallowedTools.\nallowed-tools pre-approves; it does NOT block other tools.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: To remove capability use deny rules/disallowed tools; to avoid prompts use allowed tools.\n\nEXAMPLE: A read-only review skill lists Read, Grep and Bash(git diff:*) as allowed AND denies Edit/Write elsewhere.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Assuming allowed-tools is a whitelist that blocks everything else leaves other tools available under normal permissions.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-382","type":"rectangle","x":0,"y":5578.76,"width":1150,"height":251.76000000000002,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":383,"version":1,"versionNonce":384,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-385","type":"text","x":16,"y":5590.76,"width":1118,"height":23,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":386,"version":1,"versionNonce":387,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"9 · PLAN MODE  vs  DIRECT EXECUTION","rawText":"9 · PLAN MODE  vs  DIRECT EXECUTION","fontSize":17,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":17,"containerId":null,"originalText":"9 · PLAN MODE  vs  DIRECT EXECUTION","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-388","type":"text","x":16,"y":5621.76,"width":1118,"height":188.76000000000002,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":389,"version":1,"versionNonce":390,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: How large and uncertain is the change?\n\nLOOK FOR → PLAN: 'many files', 'architectural', 'multiple valid approaches', 'unknown scope', 'monolith to microservices', 'library migration 45 files'.\nLOOK FOR → DIRECT: 'single file', 'clear stack trace', 'one-line fix', 'rename across files with a known target'.\nPlan mode costs extra exploration and review.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Large/uncertain/high-blast-radius → plan. Small/well-scoped → direct. If a plan already exists → execute.\n\nEXAMPLE: Fix a null check on line 42: direct. Split the monolith: plan first, then execute.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Planning a three-line fix wastes tokens; diving into a large restructure and 'discovering' boundaries causes rework.","rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: How large and uncertain is the change?\n\nLOOK FOR → PLAN: 'many files', 'architectural', 'multiple valid approaches', 'unknown scope', 'monolith to microservices', 'library migration 45 files'.\nLOOK FOR → DIRECT: 'single file', 'clear stack trace', 'one-line fix', 'rename across files with a known target'.\nPlan mode costs extra exploration and review.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Large/uncertain/high-blast-radius → plan. Small/well-scoped → direct. If a plan already exists → execute.\n\nEXAMPLE: Fix a null check on line 42: direct. Split the monolith: plan first, then execute.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Planning a three-line fix wastes tokens; diving into a large restructure and 'discovering' boundaries causes rework.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: How large and uncertain is the change?\n\nLOOK FOR → PLAN: 'many files', 'architectural', 'multiple valid approaches', 'unknown scope', 'monolith to microservices', 'library migration 45 files'.\nLOOK FOR → DIRECT: 'single file', 'clear stack trace', 'one-line fix', 'rename across files with a known target'.\nPlan mode costs extra exploration and review.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Large/uncertain/high-blast-radius → plan. Small/well-scoped → direct. If a plan already exists → execute.\n\nEXAMPLE: Fix a null check on line 42: direct. Split the monolith: plan first, then execute.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Planning a three-line fix wastes tokens; diving into a large restructure and 'discovering' boundaries causes rework.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-391","type":"rectangle","x":1190,"y":5578.76,"width":1150,"height":251.76000000000002,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":392,"version":1,"versionNonce":393,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-394","type":"text","x":1206,"y":5590.76,"width":1118,"height":23,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":395,"version":1,"versionNonce":396,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"10 · PLAN MODE  vs  TEST-DRIVEN ITERATION","rawText":"10 · PLAN MODE  vs  TEST-DRIVEN ITERATION","fontSize":17,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":17,"containerId":null,"originalText":"10 · PLAN MODE  vs  TEST-DRIVEN ITERATION","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-397","type":"text","x":1206,"y":5621.76,"width":1118,"height":188.76000000000002,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":398,"version":1,"versionNonce":399,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Is the problem an unclear approach, or a correctness gap on specific edge cases?\n\nLOOK FOR → PLAN MODE: 'unclear approach', 'architectural ambiguity'. LOOK FOR → TESTS: 'specific edge cases fail', 'inconsistent behavior', 'prose fixes\nalready failed'.\nA reviewed plan still leaves edge cases unverified.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: If you can describe the failing inputs/outputs → write tests and iterate to green. If you can't decide how to structure the work → plan.\n\nEXAMPLE: A parser fails on three date formats. Write tests for those cases; plan mode adds nothing here.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: A plan is a specification, not a check; only executed tests give pass/fail feedback.","rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Is the problem an unclear approach, or a correctness gap on specific edge cases?\n\nLOOK FOR → PLAN MODE: 'unclear approach', 'architectural ambiguity'. LOOK FOR → TESTS: 'specific edge cases fail', 'inconsistent behavior', 'prose fixes\nalready failed'.\nA reviewed plan still leaves edge cases unverified.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: If you can describe the failing inputs/outputs → write tests and iterate to green. If you can't decide how to structure the work → plan.\n\nEXAMPLE: A parser fails on three date formats. Write tests for those cases; plan mode adds nothing here.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: A plan is a specification, not a check; only executed tests give pass/fail feedback.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Is the problem an unclear approach, or a correctness gap on specific edge cases?\n\nLOOK FOR → PLAN MODE: 'unclear approach', 'architectural ambiguity'. LOOK FOR → TESTS: 'specific edge cases fail', 'inconsistent behavior', 'prose fixes\nalready failed'.\nA reviewed plan still leaves edge cases unverified.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: If you can describe the failing inputs/outputs → write tests and iterate to green. If you can't decide how to structure the work → plan.\n\nEXAMPLE: A parser fails on three date formats. Write tests for those cases; plan mode adds nothing here.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: A plan is a specification, not a check; only executed tests give pass/fail feedback.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-400","type":"rectangle","x":0,"y":5854.52,"width":1150,"height":251.76000000000002,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":401,"version":1,"versionNonce":402,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-403","type":"text","x":16,"y":5866.52,"width":1118,"height":23,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":404,"version":1,"versionNonce":405,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"11 · Explore SUBAGENT  vs  PLAN MODE  vs  MAIN SESSION","rawText":"11 · Explore SUBAGENT  vs  PLAN MODE  vs  MAIN SESSION","fontSize":17,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":17,"containerId":null,"originalText":"11 · Explore SUBAGENT  vs  PLAN MODE  vs  MAIN SESSION","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-406","type":"text","x":16,"y":5897.52,"width":1118,"height":188.76000000000002,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":407,"version":1,"versionNonce":408,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Is the goal to LEARN about the code, DECIDE an approach, or CHANGE code?\n\nLOOK FOR → EXPLORE: 'where is X defined', 'how does Y work', verbose discovery. LOOK FOR → PLAN: 'decide how to restructure'. LOOK FOR → MAIN/DIRECT: 'make\nthe change'.\nExplore is read-only and returns a summary.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Discovery first (Explore) → approach (plan mode) → edits (execute) → commit.\n\nEXAMPLE: 'Understand how auth works' → Explore returns a summary; then plan the change.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Reading dozens of files in the main session floods context; using Explore to make edits is impossible (read-only).","rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Is the goal to LEARN about the code, DECIDE an approach, or CHANGE code?\n\nLOOK FOR → EXPLORE: 'where is X defined', 'how does Y work', verbose discovery. LOOK FOR → PLAN: 'decide how to restructure'. LOOK FOR → MAIN/DIRECT: 'make\nthe change'.\nExplore is read-only and returns a summary.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Discovery first (Explore) → approach (plan mode) → edits (execute) → commit.\n\nEXAMPLE: 'Understand how auth works' → Explore returns a summary; then plan the change.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Reading dozens of files in the main session floods context; using Explore to make edits is impossible (read-only).","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Is the goal to LEARN about the code, DECIDE an approach, or CHANGE code?\n\nLOOK FOR → EXPLORE: 'where is X defined', 'how does Y work', verbose discovery. LOOK FOR → PLAN: 'decide how to restructure'. LOOK FOR → MAIN/DIRECT: 'make\nthe change'.\nExplore is read-only and returns a summary.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Discovery first (Explore) → approach (plan mode) → edits (execute) → commit.\n\nEXAMPLE: 'Understand how auth works' → Explore returns a summary; then plan the change.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Reading dozens of files in the main session floods context; using Explore to make edits is impossible (read-only).","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-409","type":"rectangle","x":1190,"y":5854.52,"width":1150,"height":251.76000000000002,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":410,"version":1,"versionNonce":411,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-412","type":"text","x":1206,"y":5866.52,"width":1118,"height":23,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":413,"version":1,"versionNonce":414,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"12 · EXAMPLES  vs  PROSE  vs  EMPHASIS MARKERS","rawText":"12 · EXAMPLES  vs  PROSE  vs  EMPHASIS MARKERS","fontSize":17,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":17,"containerId":null,"originalText":"12 · EXAMPLES  vs  PROSE  vs  EMPHASIS MARKERS","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-415","type":"text","x":1206,"y":5897.52,"width":1118,"height":188.76000000000002,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":416,"version":1,"versionNonce":417,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Is the output shape ambiguous, and have prose attempts already failed?\n\nLOOK FOR → EXAMPLES: 'inconsistent format', 'ambiguous instructions', 'extraction/transformation'. LOOK FOR → WRONG FIXES: 'add IMPORTANT in bold', 'repeat\nthe rule', 'temperature 0'.\nEmphasis raises priority, not clarity.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Show 2–3 input/output pairs (or tests). Don't rewrite prose again.\n\nEXAMPLE: Citations alternate between inline and footnote styles. Provide two example outputs in the desired style.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Bold/CAPS/repetition doesn't resolve ambiguity or add a feedback loop; temperature controls randomness, not format understanding.","rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Is the output shape ambiguous, and have prose attempts already failed?\n\nLOOK FOR → EXAMPLES: 'inconsistent format', 'ambiguous instructions', 'extraction/transformation'. LOOK FOR → WRONG FIXES: 'add IMPORTANT in bold', 'repeat\nthe rule', 'temperature 0'.\nEmphasis raises priority, not clarity.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Show 2–3 input/output pairs (or tests). Don't rewrite prose again.\n\nEXAMPLE: Citations alternate between inline and footnote styles. Provide two example outputs in the desired style.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Bold/CAPS/repetition doesn't resolve ambiguity or add a feedback loop; temperature controls randomness, not format understanding.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Is the output shape ambiguous, and have prose attempts already failed?\n\nLOOK FOR → EXAMPLES: 'inconsistent format', 'ambiguous instructions', 'extraction/transformation'. LOOK FOR → WRONG FIXES: 'add IMPORTANT in bold', 'repeat\nthe rule', 'temperature 0'.\nEmphasis raises priority, not clarity.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Show 2–3 input/output pairs (or tests). Don't rewrite prose again.\n\nEXAMPLE: Citations alternate between inline and footnote styles. Provide two example outputs in the desired style.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Bold/CAPS/repetition doesn't resolve ambiguity or add a feedback loop; temperature controls randomness, not format understanding.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-418","type":"rectangle","x":0,"y":6130.280000000001,"width":1150,"height":251.76000000000002,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":419,"version":1,"versionNonce":420,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-421","type":"text","x":16,"y":6142.280000000001,"width":1118,"height":23,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":422,"version":1,"versionNonce":423,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"13 · TEST-DRIVEN ITERATION  vs  INTERVIEW PATTERN","rawText":"13 · TEST-DRIVEN ITERATION  vs  INTERVIEW PATTERN","fontSize":17,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":17,"containerId":null,"originalText":"13 · TEST-DRIVEN ITERATION  vs  INTERVIEW PATTERN","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-424","type":"text","x":16,"y":6173.280000000001,"width":1118,"height":188.76000000000002,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":425,"version":1,"versionNonce":426,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Do you KNOW the edge cases, or do you not know what you don't know?\n\nLOOK FOR → TEST-DRIVEN: 'known edge cases', 'regression', 'need precise failure feedback'. LOOK FOR → INTERVIEW: 'unfamiliar domain', 'up-front specs keep\nmissing unanticipated failure modes', 'design caching/auth/billing'.\nInterview discovers requirements; tests verify them.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Known cases → tests. Unknown unknowns → Claude interviews you first, you consolidate a spec, then tests.\n\nEXAMPLE: Designing a caching layer: interview (eviction, invalidation, failure modes, memory budget) → spec → tests.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Writing tests for cases you haven't thought of is impossible; interviewing doesn't verify behavior.","rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Do you KNOW the edge cases, or do you not know what you don't know?\n\nLOOK FOR → TEST-DRIVEN: 'known edge cases', 'regression', 'need precise failure feedback'. LOOK FOR → INTERVIEW: 'unfamiliar domain', 'up-front specs keep\nmissing unanticipated failure modes', 'design caching/auth/billing'.\nInterview discovers requirements; tests verify them.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Known cases → tests. Unknown unknowns → Claude interviews you first, you consolidate a spec, then tests.\n\nEXAMPLE: Designing a caching layer: interview (eviction, invalidation, failure modes, memory budget) → spec → tests.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Writing tests for cases you haven't thought of is impossible; interviewing doesn't verify behavior.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Do you KNOW the edge cases, or do you not know what you don't know?\n\nLOOK FOR → TEST-DRIVEN: 'known edge cases', 'regression', 'need precise failure feedback'. LOOK FOR → INTERVIEW: 'unfamiliar domain', 'up-front specs keep\nmissing unanticipated failure modes', 'design caching/auth/billing'.\nInterview discovers requirements; tests verify them.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Known cases → tests. Unknown unknowns → Claude interviews you first, you consolidate a spec, then tests.\n\nEXAMPLE: Designing a caching layer: interview (eviction, invalidation, failure modes, memory budget) → spec → tests.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Writing tests for cases you haven't thought of is impossible; interviewing doesn't verify behavior.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-427","type":"rectangle","x":1190,"y":6130.280000000001,"width":1150,"height":251.76000000000002,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":428,"version":1,"versionNonce":429,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-430","type":"text","x":1206,"y":6142.280000000001,"width":1118,"height":23,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":431,"version":1,"versionNonce":432,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"14 · BATCH FIXES  vs  SEQUENTIAL FIXES","rawText":"14 · BATCH FIXES  vs  SEQUENTIAL FIXES","fontSize":17,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":17,"containerId":null,"originalText":"14 · BATCH FIXES  vs  SEQUENTIAL FIXES","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-433","type":"text","x":1206,"y":6173.280000000001,"width":1118,"height":188.76000000000002,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":434,"version":1,"versionNonce":435,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Do the problems interact?\n\nLOOK FOR → BATCH: 'changing one affects the others' (date format affects parsing, display, DB). LOOK FOR → SEQUENCE: 'independent', 'unrelated typos and\nnull checks'.\nInteracting fixes need joint reasoning; independent fixes need small, attributable diffs.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Interacting → one detailed message. Independent → one per turn.\n\nEXAMPLE: Date-format change across three modules: one message. A typo and an unrelated null check: two turns.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Splitting interacting fixes causes conflicting changes; batching independent fixes hides which change broke something.","rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Do the problems interact?\n\nLOOK FOR → BATCH: 'changing one affects the others' (date format affects parsing, display, DB). LOOK FOR → SEQUENCE: 'independent', 'unrelated typos and\nnull checks'.\nInteracting fixes need joint reasoning; independent fixes need small, attributable diffs.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Interacting → one detailed message. Independent → one per turn.\n\nEXAMPLE: Date-format change across three modules: one message. A typo and an unrelated null check: two turns.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Splitting interacting fixes causes conflicting changes; batching independent fixes hides which change broke something.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Do the problems interact?\n\nLOOK FOR → BATCH: 'changing one affects the others' (date format affects parsing, display, DB). LOOK FOR → SEQUENCE: 'independent', 'unrelated typos and\nnull checks'.\nInteracting fixes need joint reasoning; independent fixes need small, attributable diffs.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Interacting → one detailed message. Independent → one per turn.\n\nEXAMPLE: Date-format change across three modules: one message. A typo and an unrelated null check: two turns.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Splitting interacting fixes causes conflicting changes; batching independent fixes hides which change broke something.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-436","type":"rectangle","x":0,"y":6406.040000000001,"width":1150,"height":234.6,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":437,"version":1,"versionNonce":438,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-439","type":"text","x":16,"y":6418.040000000001,"width":1118,"height":23,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":440,"version":1,"versionNonce":441,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"15 · claude -p  vs  CLAUDE_HEADLESS / --batch / </dev/null","rawText":"15 · claude -p  vs  CLAUDE_HEADLESS / --batch / </dev/null","fontSize":17,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":17,"containerId":null,"originalText":"15 · claude -p  vs  CLAUDE_HEADLESS / --batch / </dev/null","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-442","type":"text","x":16,"y":6449.040000000001,"width":1118,"height":171.6,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":443,"version":1,"versionNonce":444,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: How do you run Claude Code unattended in CI?\n\nLOOK FOR → -p: 'non-interactive', 'pipeline hangs'. LOOK FOR → DISTRACTORS: 'CLAUDE_HEADLESS=true', '--batch', 'redirect stdin from /dev/null'.\nOnly -p/--print is the documented non-interactive mode.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Pick -p. Then add --output-format json (and --json-schema) when a script must parse the result.\n\nEXAMPLE: A GitHub Action hangs waiting for input → add -p.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: The distractors sound plausible but Claude Code still expects an interactive UI.","rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: How do you run Claude Code unattended in CI?\n\nLOOK FOR → -p: 'non-interactive', 'pipeline hangs'. LOOK FOR → DISTRACTORS: 'CLAUDE_HEADLESS=true', '--batch', 'redirect stdin from /dev/null'.\nOnly -p/--print is the documented non-interactive mode.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Pick -p. Then add --output-format json (and --json-schema) when a script must parse the result.\n\nEXAMPLE: A GitHub Action hangs waiting for input → add -p.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: The distractors sound plausible but Claude Code still expects an interactive UI.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: How do you run Claude Code unattended in CI?\n\nLOOK FOR → -p: 'non-interactive', 'pipeline hangs'. LOOK FOR → DISTRACTORS: 'CLAUDE_HEADLESS=true', '--batch', 'redirect stdin from /dev/null'.\nOnly -p/--print is the documented non-interactive mode.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Pick -p. Then add --output-format json (and --json-schema) when a script must parse the result.\n\nEXAMPLE: A GitHub Action hangs waiting for input → add -p.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: The distractors sound plausible but Claude Code still expects an interactive UI.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-445","type":"rectangle","x":1190,"y":6406.040000000001,"width":1150,"height":234.6,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":446,"version":1,"versionNonce":447,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-448","type":"text","x":1206,"y":6418.040000000001,"width":1118,"height":23,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":449,"version":1,"versionNonce":450,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"16 · --json-schema  vs  'PLEASE REPLY IN JSON'","rawText":"16 · --json-schema  vs  'PLEASE REPLY IN JSON'","fontSize":17,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":17,"containerId":null,"originalText":"16 · --json-schema  vs  'PLEASE REPLY IN JSON'","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-451","type":"text","x":1206,"y":6449.040000000001,"width":1118,"height":171.6,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":452,"version":1,"versionNonce":453,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Must the output be machine-parseable and validated?\n\nLOOK FOR → --json-schema: 'post inline PR comments', 'downstream step parses findings', 'validated structure'. LOOK FOR → PROSE: 'reply in JSON please'.\nSchema validation is enforced; prose requests are not.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Use --output-format json with --json-schema when another step consumes the output.\n\nEXAMPLE: Findings with path, line, severity, message → each becomes an inline GitHub comment.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: A prose request can still produce extra text or missing fields that break the next step.","rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Must the output be machine-parseable and validated?\n\nLOOK FOR → --json-schema: 'post inline PR comments', 'downstream step parses findings', 'validated structure'. LOOK FOR → PROSE: 'reply in JSON please'.\nSchema validation is enforced; prose requests are not.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Use --output-format json with --json-schema when another step consumes the output.\n\nEXAMPLE: Findings with path, line, severity, message → each becomes an inline GitHub comment.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: A prose request can still produce extra text or missing fields that break the next step.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Must the output be machine-parseable and validated?\n\nLOOK FOR → --json-schema: 'post inline PR comments', 'downstream step parses findings', 'validated structure'. LOOK FOR → PROSE: 'reply in JSON please'.\nSchema validation is enforced; prose requests are not.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Use --output-format json with --json-schema when another step consumes the output.\n\nEXAMPLE: Findings with path, line, severity, message → each becomes an inline GitHub comment.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: A prose request can still produce extra text or missing fields that break the next step.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-454","type":"rectangle","x":0,"y":6664.640000000001,"width":1150,"height":234.6,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":455,"version":1,"versionNonce":456,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-457","type":"text","x":16,"y":6676.640000000001,"width":1118,"height":23,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":458,"version":1,"versionNonce":459,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"17 · REAL-TIME (SYNC)  vs  MESSAGE BATCHES API","rawText":"17 · REAL-TIME (SYNC)  vs  MESSAGE BATCHES API","fontSize":17,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":17,"containerId":null,"originalText":"17 · REAL-TIME (SYNC)  vs  MESSAGE BATCHES API","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-460","type":"text","x":16,"y":6707.640000000001,"width":1118,"height":171.6,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":461,"version":1,"versionNonce":462,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Does something wait on the result?\n\nLOOK FOR → SYNC: 'blocks the merge', 'pre-merge check'. LOOK FOR → BATCH: 'overnight', 'nightly report', 'tech-debt report', 'cost savings OK'.\nBatches API: ~50% cheaper but up to ~24 hours.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: If a human or pipeline is blocked waiting → synchronous. If results can arrive later → batch.\n\nEXAMPLE: Pre-merge security gate: real-time call. Overnight tech-debt report: batch.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Batch for a blocking gate stalls merges; sync for overnight work overpays.","rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Does something wait on the result?\n\nLOOK FOR → SYNC: 'blocks the merge', 'pre-merge check'. LOOK FOR → BATCH: 'overnight', 'nightly report', 'tech-debt report', 'cost savings OK'.\nBatches API: ~50% cheaper but up to ~24 hours.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: If a human or pipeline is blocked waiting → synchronous. If results can arrive later → batch.\n\nEXAMPLE: Pre-merge security gate: real-time call. Overnight tech-debt report: batch.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Batch for a blocking gate stalls merges; sync for overnight work overpays.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Does something wait on the result?\n\nLOOK FOR → SYNC: 'blocks the merge', 'pre-merge check'. LOOK FOR → BATCH: 'overnight', 'nightly report', 'tech-debt report', 'cost savings OK'.\nBatches API: ~50% cheaper but up to ~24 hours.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: If a human or pipeline is blocked waiting → synchronous. If results can arrive later → batch.\n\nEXAMPLE: Pre-merge security gate: real-time call. Overnight tech-debt report: batch.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Batch for a blocking gate stalls merges; sync for overnight work overpays.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-463","type":"rectangle","x":1190,"y":6664.640000000001,"width":1150,"height":251.76000000000002,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":464,"version":1,"versionNonce":465,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-466","type":"text","x":1206,"y":6676.640000000001,"width":1118,"height":23,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":467,"version":1,"versionNonce":468,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"18 · SAME SESSION REVIEWS ITS OWN CODE  vs  INDEPENDENT REVIEW SESSION","rawText":"18 · SAME SESSION REVIEWS ITS OWN CODE  vs  INDEPENDENT REVIEW SESSION","fontSize":17,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":17,"containerId":null,"originalText":"18 · SAME SESSION REVIEWS ITS OWN CODE  vs  INDEPENDENT REVIEW SESSION","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-469","type":"text","x":1206,"y":6707.640000000001,"width":1118,"height":188.76000000000002,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":470,"version":1,"versionNonce":471,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Is the reviewer biased by having written the code?\n\nLOOK FOR → INDEPENDENT: 'reviewer misses what the author anchored on', 'self-review', 'confirms its own decisions'. LOOK FOR → SAME SESSION: only for quick\nself-checks.\nThe generating session carries its reasoning and biases.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Start a fresh session (new --session-id) with only the diff and CLAUDE.md. Never --continue from the implementation session.\n\nEXAMPLE: Code was written in session A; review runs in fresh session B and catches an issue A rationalized away.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: A bigger model doesn't remove the author's context bias; resuming the same session keeps it.","rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Is the reviewer biased by having written the code?\n\nLOOK FOR → INDEPENDENT: 'reviewer misses what the author anchored on', 'self-review', 'confirms its own decisions'. LOOK FOR → SAME SESSION: only for quick\nself-checks.\nThe generating session carries its reasoning and biases.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Start a fresh session (new --session-id) with only the diff and CLAUDE.md. Never --continue from the implementation session.\n\nEXAMPLE: Code was written in session A; review runs in fresh session B and catches an issue A rationalized away.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: A bigger model doesn't remove the author's context bias; resuming the same session keeps it.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Is the reviewer biased by having written the code?\n\nLOOK FOR → INDEPENDENT: 'reviewer misses what the author anchored on', 'self-review', 'confirms its own decisions'. LOOK FOR → SAME SESSION: only for quick\nself-checks.\nThe generating session carries its reasoning and biases.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Start a fresh session (new --session-id) with only the diff and CLAUDE.md. Never --continue from the implementation session.\n\nEXAMPLE: Code was written in session A; review runs in fresh session B and catches an issue A rationalized away.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: A bigger model doesn't remove the author's context bias; resuming the same session keeps it.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-472","type":"rectangle","x":0,"y":6940.4000000000015,"width":1150,"height":234.6,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":473,"version":1,"versionNonce":474,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-475","type":"text","x":16,"y":6952.4000000000015,"width":1118,"height":23,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":476,"version":1,"versionNonce":477,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"19 · PER-FILE + INTEGRATION PASS  vs  ONE BIG PASS","rawText":"19 · PER-FILE + INTEGRATION PASS  vs  ONE BIG PASS","fontSize":17,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":17,"containerId":null,"originalText":"19 · PER-FILE + INTEGRATION PASS  vs  ONE BIG PASS","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-478","type":"text","x":16,"y":6983.4000000000015,"width":1118,"height":171.6,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":479,"version":1,"versionNonce":480,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: How big is the PR?\n\nLOOK FOR → TWO PASSES: '14 files', 'inconsistent verdicts', 'misses cross-file issues'. LOOK FOR → ONE PASS: small PR.\nAttention dilution is an allocation problem, not a token-budget problem.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Large → per-file passes (local) + one integration pass (cross-file). Small → one pass.\n\nEXAMPLE: 14-file PR: 14 focused reviews, then one pass on API boundaries, migrations and contract drift.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: One mega-prompt gives uneven depth; a bigger window doesn't fix attention; per-file only misses cross-file bugs.","rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: How big is the PR?\n\nLOOK FOR → TWO PASSES: '14 files', 'inconsistent verdicts', 'misses cross-file issues'. LOOK FOR → ONE PASS: small PR.\nAttention dilution is an allocation problem, not a token-budget problem.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Large → per-file passes (local) + one integration pass (cross-file). Small → one pass.\n\nEXAMPLE: 14-file PR: 14 focused reviews, then one pass on API boundaries, migrations and contract drift.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: One mega-prompt gives uneven depth; a bigger window doesn't fix attention; per-file only misses cross-file bugs.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: How big is the PR?\n\nLOOK FOR → TWO PASSES: '14 files', 'inconsistent verdicts', 'misses cross-file issues'. LOOK FOR → ONE PASS: small PR.\nAttention dilution is an allocation problem, not a token-budget problem.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Large → per-file passes (local) + one integration pass (cross-file). Small → one pass.\n\nEXAMPLE: 14-file PR: 14 focused reviews, then one pass on API boundaries, migrations and contract drift.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: One mega-prompt gives uneven depth; a bigger window doesn't fix attention; per-file only misses cross-file bugs.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-481","type":"rectangle","x":1190,"y":6940.4000000000015,"width":1150,"height":234.6,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":482,"version":1,"versionNonce":483,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-484","type":"text","x":1206,"y":6952.4000000000015,"width":1118,"height":23,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":485,"version":1,"versionNonce":486,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"20 · RE-REVIEW WITH PRIOR FINDINGS  vs  FRESH REVIEW EVERY PUSH","rawText":"20 · RE-REVIEW WITH PRIOR FINDINGS  vs  FRESH REVIEW EVERY PUSH","fontSize":17,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":17,"containerId":null,"originalText":"20 · RE-REVIEW WITH PRIOR FINDINGS  vs  FRESH REVIEW EVERY PUSH","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-487","type":"text","x":1206,"y":6983.4000000000015,"width":1118,"height":171.6,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":488,"version":1,"versionNonce":489,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Are reviewers spamming duplicate comments?\n\nLOOK FOR → PRIOR FINDINGS: 'duplicate comments on every push'. LOOK FOR → FRESH: first-time review.\nPass what's already been said.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: On re-push, feed previous comments and ask for only new or unresolved issues (resume the review session or attach the comments).\n\nEXAMPLE: Second push: the reviewer posts 2 new comments instead of repeating 9 old ones.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: A fresh review with no history re-reports everything; asking the model to 'avoid duplicates' without the history can't work.","rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Are reviewers spamming duplicate comments?\n\nLOOK FOR → PRIOR FINDINGS: 'duplicate comments on every push'. LOOK FOR → FRESH: first-time review.\nPass what's already been said.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: On re-push, feed previous comments and ask for only new or unresolved issues (resume the review session or attach the comments).\n\nEXAMPLE: Second push: the reviewer posts 2 new comments instead of repeating 9 old ones.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: A fresh review with no history re-reports everything; asking the model to 'avoid duplicates' without the history can't work.","fontSize":13,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":13,"containerId":null,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Are reviewers spamming duplicate comments?\n\nLOOK FOR → PRIOR FINDINGS: 'duplicate comments on every push'. LOOK FOR → FRESH: first-time review.\nPass what's already been said.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: On re-push, feed previous comments and ask for only new or unresolved issues (resume the review session or attach the comments).\n\nEXAMPLE: Second push: the reviewer posts 2 new comments instead of repeating 9 old ones.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: A fresh review with no history re-reports everything; asking the model to 'avoid duplicates' without the history can't work.","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"t-490","type":"text","x":0,"y":7219.000000000002,"width":2400,"height":36,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f3d8a","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":null,"seed":491,"version":1,"versionNonce":492,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false,"text":"FAILURE / DECISION MATRIX","rawText":"FAILURE / DECISION MATRIX","fontSize":26,"fontFamily":2,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","baseline":26,"containerId":null,"originalText":"FAILURE / DECISION MATRIX","lineHeight":1.25},{"id":"r-493","type":"rectangle","x":0,"y":7267.000000000002,"width":1500,"height":42,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#0f3d8a","backgroundColor":"#0f3d8a","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"groupIds":[],"frameId":null,"roundness":{"type":3},"seed":494,"version":1,"versionNonce":495,"isDeleted":false,"boundElements":null,"updated":1792015114101,"link":null,"locked":false},{"id":"t-496","type":"text","x":16,"y":7278.000000000002,"width":740,"height":26,"angle":0,"strokeColor":"#ffffff","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opa